{:edb/db-before-t 2555 :edb/db-after-t 2556 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535868 50 #inst "2026-10-15T02:41:50.163Z" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186053913 1075 #edb/ref 17592186053914 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186053913 1075 #edb/ref 17592186053915 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186053913 1075 #edb/ref 17592186053916 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186053913 1075 #edb/ref 17592186053917 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186053913 1075 #edb/ref 17592186053918 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186053913 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534447 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186053913 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534508 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186053913 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534528 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186053913 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534594 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186053913 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534802 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186053913 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534447 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054514 1071 "![](images/3f/3f162b6b0c51a94c3c752f8a6ee59d4232c5bbb2fe619c7c6273f37805c281b0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. What are the coordinates of the point $P$?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186054514 1071 "![](images/3f/3f162b6b0c51a94c3c752f8a6ee59d4232c5bbb2fe619c7c6273f37805c281b0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. What are the coordinates of the point $P?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054514 1074 "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n\\theta=45^\\circ\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\cos 45^{∘} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\\\\ny &= \\sin 45^{∘} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186054514 1074 "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta=45^\\circ$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right).$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054515 1071 "![](images/56/56adc0846dcc0a0f6352eb4ce411f4fffdcd6f91e20524c83bd70af1bd4d948c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$, what is the measure of the angle $\\theta$?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186054515 1071 "![](images/56/56adc0846dcc0a0f6352eb4ce411f4fffdcd6f91e20524c83bd70af1bd4d948c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2},$ what is the measure of the angle $\\theta?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054515 1074 "We're given that\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\nWe compute the angle $\\theta$ using the inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arcsin\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right) = 60^\\circ\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186054515 1074 "We're given that $y=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute the angle $\\theta$ using the inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arcsin\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right) = 60^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054516 1071 "![](images/1b/1b084e0ad229e935d70d751f0e0ddaacf5832017951a7ef8e334f27834662d5c.png)\n\nThe point $P\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7},\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\\right)$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\tan\\theta?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054516 1071 "![](images/1b/1b084e0ad229e935d70d751f0e0ddaacf5832017951a7ef8e334f27834662d5c.png)\n\nThe point $P\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7},\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\\right)$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\tan\\theta$?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186054516 1074 "We're given that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7}, \\qquad \\sin\\theta =\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\n$$\n\nKnowing that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $\\tan\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{\\sqrt{7}} = \\sqrt 6\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186054516 1074 "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7}$ and $y=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7}, \\qquad \\sin\\theta =\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos\\theta},$ we can calculate $\\tan\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{42}}{7}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 7}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{42} }{\\sqrt{7}} = \\sqrt 6.\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054517 1071 "![](images/16/1635b5c2b1c515601e5339a0814132fca63164e7ab2b45c93ea5c2b3f5e0358b.png)\n\nThe point $P\\left( \\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3},\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\sec\\theta?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054517 1071 "![](images/16/1635b5c2b1c515601e5339a0814132fca63164e7ab2b45c93ea5c2b3f5e0358b.png)\n\nThe point $P\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3},\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\sec\\theta$?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186054517 1074 "We're given that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}\n$$\n\nKnowing that\n\n$$\n\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $\\sec\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}\\right)} =\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt 8} = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt 8}{8}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186054517 1074 "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta},$ we can calculate $\\sec\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sec\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 8}{3}\\right)} =\\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt 8} = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt 8}{8}.\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054609 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535039 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054609 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535088 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054716 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535005 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054716 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535067 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054717 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534980 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534971 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534982 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534990 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535064 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535037 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054740 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535063 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054741 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535066 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186054870 1050 660.0 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186055446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186055446 1071 "![](images/19/198f3a8a98e9650cf53c81a24a4fc96e08a743bdd6c48e47eb4b59f5bc8e4f2c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$, what is the measure of the angle $θ$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186055446 1071 "![](images/19/198f3a8a98e9650cf53c81a24a4fc96e08a743bdd6c48e47eb4b59f5bc8e4f2c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ what is the measure of the angle $\\theta ?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186055446 1074 "![](images/5b/5b8cfbcd58092a40f34ed969e9772981615db54eb5c20c5b7543e038a6e0fade.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute the angle $\\theta $ using the inverse cosine:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186055959 1064 "Let's consider the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ in the first quadrant, measured in the usual way.\n\n![](images/3d/3d51ca417ba4f6f62bd3e5db5997842d0671f18920a7de70578146a49de79ec3.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the $x$-coordinate of $P$ coincides with the cosine of the angle $\\theta$, and\n- the $y$-coordinate coincides with the sine of the angle $\\theta$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nConsequently, if we know the angle $\\theta$, we can easily work out the coordinates of the point $P$, and vice-versa. This works regardless of whether the angle $\\theta$ is given in degrees or radians.\n\nFor now, we'll focus on points located in the first quadrant. Later, we will encounter points in all quadrants." 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186055959 1064 "Let's consider the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ in the first quadrant, measured in the usual way.\n\n![](images/bc/bca60509a15e4d32d06d96c7cf5eac65a00b4e5cb72242152dd998b933209d7d.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the $x$-coordinate of $P$ coincides with the cosine of the angle $\\theta,$ and\n\n- the $y$-coordinate coincides with the sine of the angle $\\theta.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nConsequently, if we know the angle $\\theta,$ we can easily work out the coordinates of the point $P,$ and vice-versa. This works regardless of whether the angle $\\theta$ is given in degrees or radians.\n\nFor now, we'll focus on points located in the first quadrant. Later, we will encounter points in all quadrants." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186055960 1064 "Let's consider the unit circle and a point $P(x,y)$ that lies on the circle.\n\n![](images/3d/3d51ca417ba4f6f62bd3e5db5997842d0671f18920a7de70578146a49de79ec3.png)\n\nTo prove that\n\n$$\nx=\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nlet's examine the triangle that's formed by $\\overline{OP}$ and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/53/53d84bedcddcb484bc613a2f01e002afe16b614daa909ff744195bcc45464472.png)\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The hypotenuse has length $1$ since it is a radius of the unit circle.\n- The adjacent and opposite sides have lengths $x$ and $y$, respectively.\n\nTherefore, using the definitions of $\\cos\\theta$ and $\\sin\\theta$, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{x}{1} = x, \\qquad \\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{1} = y\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186055960 1064 "Let's consider the unit circle and a point $P(x,y)$ that lies on the circle.\n\n![](images/3d/3d51ca417ba4f6f62bd3e5db5997842d0671f18920a7de70578146a49de79ec3.png)\n\nTo prove that $x=\\cos\\theta$ and $y=\\sin\\theta,$ let's examine the triangle that's formed by $\\overline{OP}$ and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/53/53d84bedcddcb484bc613a2f01e002afe16b614daa909ff744195bcc45464472.png)\n\n  \n\nNotice that: \n\n- The hypotenuse has length $1$ since it is a radius of the unit circle.\n\n- The adjacent and opposite sides have lengths $x$ and $y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, using the definitions of $\\cos\\theta$ and $\\sin\\theta$, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{x}{1} = x, \\qquad  \\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{1} = y.\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186055963 1064 "To find the tangent of an angle using the unit circle, we need to use the relationship\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nTo see why this is true, let's take a look at the following triangle.\n\n![](images/99/99b51d7d25a266dbfda7839be6bafc335b181ecd6dc74224fcb0557180f872aa.png)\n\nFor this particular triangle, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{z}, \\qquad \\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{x}{z}, \\qquad \\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nWhen we take $\\sin\\theta$ and divide it by $\\cos\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sin θ}{\\cos θ} &= \\frac{y}{z} \\div \\frac{x}{z} \\\\\n&= \\frac{y}{z} \\cdot \\frac{z}{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{y \\cdot z}{x \\cdot z} \\\\\n&= \\frac{y \\cdot z}{x \\cdot z} \\\\\n&= \\frac{y}{x} \\\\\n&= \\tan θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough this proof is only for acute angles, the identity is true for any angle, provided that $\\tan\\theta$ is well-defined.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan\\theta}\n$$\n\nit immediately follows that\n\n$$\n\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\\right)} = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186055963 1064 "To find the tangent of an angle using the unit circle, we need to use the relationship\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTo see why this is true, let's take a look at the following triangle.\n\n![](images/99/99b51d7d25a266dbfda7839be6bafc335b181ecd6dc74224fcb0557180f872aa.png)\n\nFor this particular triangle, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{z}, \\qquad \\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{x}{z}, \\qquad \\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nWhen we take $\\sin\\theta$ and divide it by $\\cos\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{y}{z}÷\\frac{x}{z} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{z}\\cdot \\frac{z}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\cdot z}{x\\cdot z} \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\cdot \\cancel{z}}{x\\cdot \\cancel{z}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough this proof is only for acute angles, the identity is true for any angle, provided that $\\tan\\theta$ is well-defined.\n\nFinally, since $\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\tan\\theta},$ it immediately follows that\n\n$$\n\\cot\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\\right)} = \\dfrac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta}.\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186056304 1078 #edb/ref 17592186056305 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186059793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534970 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186059991 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186059991 1071 "If $(\\log_{7} (x + 1))/(2) = 1$, then $x =$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186059991 1071 "If $\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)}{2}=1$, then $x=$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186059991 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)}{2} & =1 \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x+1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(x+1) & =2 \\\\ {7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)} & ={7}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x+1)}=x+1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={7}^{2} \\\\ x+1 & =49 \\\\ x & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186064163 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535032 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186064163 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535033 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186064163 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535061 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186064164 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535053 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186066958 1078 #edb/ref 17592186066959 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186066964 1078 #edb/ref 17592186066968 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186067426 1078 #edb/ref 17592186067431 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186068553 1074 "First, let's compute the step size $\\Delta x$, which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=2$, and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\n\\Delta x=\\dfrac{b-a}{n}=\\dfrac{2-0}{4}=0.5\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our situation. We are using a left Riemann sum, which means we draw the rectangles so that the left corner of each rectangle coincides with the curve.\n\n![](images/56/56427bec8108b822c4e1980725073cedc9fc79575d606014f72ea6ca9f8959c2.png)\n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0)}}+{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $16$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA \\approx (16 + 9 + 4 + 1) \\cdot 0.5 = 15\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186068553 1074 "First, let's compute the step size $\\Delta x,$ which is just the width of 