emann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-1$ and $x=3$ for each of the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *overestimate* of the area?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186069376 1074 "The left Riemann sum overestimates the area under the respective curve if the function is decreasing over the given interval. \n\nOf the given options, the only one that is decreasing over $x\\in [-1,3]$ is the following. \n\n![](images/62/622f8fd49fb32255b29bd0ae8c36c146aeb4b48aec7be2cde69832cb33fd2d83.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186069582 1061 1400 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186069582 1061 652096 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186069583 1061 1401 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186069583 1061 652098 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186069584 1061 1404 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186069584 1061 652097 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186069585 1061 1405 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186069585 1061 652108 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186069586 1061 1406 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186069586 1061 652109 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186069720 1061 26738 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186069720 1061 652101 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186069721 1061 26741 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186069721 1061 652105 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186069722 1061 26748 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186069722 1061 652104 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186069981 1064 "Whether the left Riemann sum underestimates or overestimates the actual area under a function depends on whether the function is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is increasing, then the rectangles fall *below* the function, which means the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x)$.![](images/88/883b1d5faacf246557319e1f2b4fa253a06f657c589b24e5ec27eb35c00528c5.png)\n- If the function $f(x)$ is decreasing, then the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means the left Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/ab/ab23c5b0d95a6bd78ac337266bb504c63b528d756548cbcd38e8b236ef140bc3.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186069981 1064 "Whether the left Riemann sum underestimates or overestimates the actual area under a function depends on whether the function is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is increasing, then the rectangles fall *below* the function, which means the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/88/883b1d5faacf246557319e1f2b4fa253a06f657c589b24e5ec27eb35c00528c5.png)\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is decreasing, then  the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means the left Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n![](images/ab/ab23c5b0d95a6bd78ac337266bb504c63b528d756548cbcd38e8b236ef140bc3.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186070005 1064 "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph of $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99cee7824a157e5a08292ebba9e60a5633c7a2f8a5ebb496fc1d5dbe14f858e3.png)\n\nWe can *approximate* the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1$.\n\n![](images/8a/8aa7a3f9be8371b1459874cd2cb8c0c4caf1000d76b99d134fb6e806e638c548.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx [f(1)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(width)]^(⏞)^([math]1[/math]st rectangle) + [f(2)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(width)]^(⏞)^([math]2[/math]nd rectangle) + [f(3)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(width)]^(⏞)^([math]3[/math]rd rectangle); = [1^{2} \\cdot 1] + [2^{2} \\cdot 1] + [3^{2} \\cdot 1]; = 1 + 4 + 9; = 14\n$$\n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **left Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*left* corner of each rectangle touches the curve.\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4]$, the rectangles are entirely *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area." 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186070005 1064 "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph of $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99cee7824a157e5a08292ebba9e60a5633c7a2f8a5ebb496fc1d5dbe14f858e3.png)\n\nWe can *approximate* the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1.$\n\n![](images/8a/8aa7a3f9be8371b1459874cd2cb8c0c4caf1000d76b99d134fb6e806e638c548.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\overset{\\text{[math]1[/math]st rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]2[/math]nd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}}+\\overset{\\text{[math]3[/math]rd rectangle}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\overset{\\text{height}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}\\,]}} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{2}^{2}\\cdot 1]+[{3}^{2}\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =1+4+9 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **left Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*left* corner of each rectangle touches the curve. \n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4],$ the rectangles are entirely *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186070006 1064 "If ![](images/9a/9ac10abedf6cecc066a4858ab2859e3c3038a43b10ac3261bd4120d0dc861990.svg)