 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\theta +{sin}^{2}\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^2+y^2 = 25.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186088961 1064 "To convert the parametric equations of a circle to a Cartesian equation, we can eliminate the parameter $\\theta$ using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the circle defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=5\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^2+y^2 = 25.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186095838 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095839 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186095838 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095840 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186095838 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095841 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186095838 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095842 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186095838 1075 #edb/ref 17592186095843 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186095838 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534282 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186095838 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534315 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186095838 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534374 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186095838 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534432 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186095838 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534670 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186095838 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534315 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186096634 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534993 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186096634 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535038 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186096634 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535043 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186096635 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535081 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186096636 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535018 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186096637 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534978 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186096637 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535068 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186096853 1050 960.0 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186097319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186097319 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}p+q=1 \\\\ -2p-3q=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186097319 1071 "${\\frac{2}{5}p + q = 1; - 2p - 3q = 1$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186097319 1074 "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $5.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}p+q=1 & |\\,\\times 5 \\\\ -2p-3q=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2p+5q=5 \\\\ -2p-3q=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $2p$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-2p$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $p$-variable, and allows us to solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{2p}+5q & =5 \\\\ -\\cancel{2p}-3q & =1 \\\\ 2q & =6 \\\\ q & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $q=3$ into the equation $2p+5q=5$ to solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p+5q & =5 \\\\ 2p+5\\cdot 3 & =5 \\\\ 2p+15 & =5 \\\\ 2p & =-10 \\\\ p & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $p=-5$ and $q=3.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186097919 1064 "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with fractional coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}y &=-4 \\\\ 7x-5y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can turn the system into an equivalent system involving only integer coefficients. This will make it easier to solve.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by its lowest common denominator. In this instance, multiplying the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions $(4)$ will do the job:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}y &=-4 && |\\,\\times 4 \\\\ 7x-5y &=-2 && \\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the system using, for example, the elimination method. Notice that the $5y$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-5y$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\cancel{5y} & =-16 \\\\ 7x-\\cancel{5y} & =-2 \\\\ 9x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=-2$ into the equation $7x - 5y= -2$ to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-5y & =-2 \\\\ 7(-2)-5y & =-2 \\\\ -14-5y & =-2 \\\\ -5y & =12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2, \\,y=-\\dfrac{12}{5}.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186097919 1064 "Sometimes, we might have a system of equations with fractional coefficients, such as the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}y=-4 \\\\ 7x-5y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can turn the system into an equivalent system involving only integer coefficients. This will make it easier to solve.\n\nIn general, we multiply each equation by its lowest common denominator. In this instance, multiplying the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions $(4)$ will do the job:\n\n![](images/7f/7ff92bdf3b9c8bf5ffb571d598788f37bcd698f7b3971c337531bf9fe7f55e4e.svg)\n\nNow, we can solve the system using, for example, the elimination method. Notice that the $5y$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-5y$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\cancel{5y} & =-16 \\\\ 7x-\\cancel{5y} & =-2 \\\\ 9x & =-18 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=-2$ into the equation $7x - 5y= -2$ to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-5y & =-2 \\\\ 7(-2)-5y & =-2 \\\\ -14-5y & =-2 \\\\ -5y & =12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{12}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2, \\,y=-\\dfrac{12}{5}.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186102228 1078 #edb/ref 17592186102231 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186102258 1075 #edb/ref 17592186102259 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186102258 1075 #edb/ref 17592186102260 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186102258 1075 #edb/ref 17592186102262 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186102258 1075 #edb/ref 175921