, and then\n- draw segments connecting the image vertices.\n\nSo, our three vertices $(2,1)$, $(4,1)$, $(2,2)$ are mapped to the following points by the stretch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(2, 1) & ↦ (\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 2, 2 \\cdot 1) = (3, 2) \\\\ (4, 1) & ↦ (\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 4, 2 \\cdot 1) = (6, 2) \\\\ (2, 2) & ↦ (\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot 2, 2 \\cdot 2) = (3, 4)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following result:\n\n![](images/b9/b961a446536bddbe6ff529e7ca2973c8e957c185ef4d6f00b9255d09baa9d3cc.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186105679 1074 "To stretch a polygon, we \n\n- find the images of the vertices, and then\n\n- draw segments connecting the image vertices.\n\nSo, our three vertices $(2,1)$, $(4,1)$, $(2,2)$ are mapped to the following points by the stretch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2,1) & ↦(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2,\\,2\\cdot 1)=(3,2) \\\\ (4,1) & ↦(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 4,\\,2\\cdot 1)=(6,2) \\\\ (2,2) & ↦(\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2,\\,2\\cdot 2)=(3,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following result:\n\n![](images/b9/b961a446536bddbe6ff529e7ca2973c8e957c185ef4d6f00b9255d09baa9d3cc.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186105680 1074 "- A stretch in the $x$-direction of factor $4$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\mapsto (4x,y).\n$$\n\n- A stretch in the $y$-direction of factor $\\dfrac 1 4$ can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\mapsto \\left(x,\\dfrac 1 4y\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the combination of the stretches is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\mapsto \\left(4x,\\dfrac 1 4y\\right).\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186105680 1074 "- A stretch in the $x$-direction of factor $4$ can be represented by the function\n$(x,y)\\mapsto (4x,y)$.\n- A stretch in the $y$-direction of factor $\\dfrac 1 4$ can be represented by the function\n$(x,y)\\mapsto \\left(x,\\dfrac 1 4y\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the combination of the stretches is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\mapsto \\left(4x,\\dfrac 1 4y\\right)\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186105681 1071 "The point $(2,5)$ is stretched with invariant $y$-axis and stretch factor $2$, and then stretched with invariant $x$-axis and stretch factor $-2$. What is the resulting point?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186105681 1071 "The point $(2,5)$ is stretched with invariant $y$-axis and stretch factor $2,$ and then stretched with invariant $x$-axis and stretch factor $-2.$ What is the resulting point?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186105681 1074 "Let's apply the given transformation.\n\n- A stretch of factor $2$ and invariant $y$-axis can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(2x,y\\right).\n$$\n\n- A stretch of factor $-2$ and invariant $x$-axis can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(x,-2y\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the combination of the stretches is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left({\\color{blue}2}x,{\\color{red}-2}y\\right).\n$$\n\nApplying the combined stretch to our point, we obtain\n\n$$\n(2,5) \\mapsto \\left(2\\cdot 2, \\, -2\\cdot5 \\right) = (4,-10).\n$$\n\n![](images/0d/0d050ec1579f2ea2b37c01f2d7b60fd6580b6bc4766fcdd66005e1c2c103b8c7.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186105681 1074 "Let's apply the given transformation.\n\n- A stretch of factor $2$ and invariant $y$-axis can be represented by the function\n$(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(2x,y\\right)$.\n- A stretch of factor $-2$ and invariant $x$-axis can be represented by the function\n$(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(x,-2y\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the combination of the stretches is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left({\\color{blue}2}x,{\\color{red}-2}y\\right)\n$$\n\nApplying the combined stretch to our point, we obtain\n\n$$\n(2,5) \\mapsto \\left(2\\cdot 2, \\, -2\\cdot5 \\right) = (4,-10)\n$$\n\n![](images/0d/0d050ec1579f2ea2b37c01f2d7b60fd6580b6bc4766fcdd66005e1c2c103b8c7.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186105842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534984 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186105842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535019 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186105843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535009 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186105843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535074 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186105844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535028 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186105887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535047 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186105887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535075 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186105888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186534983 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186105889 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535084 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186105996 1050 420.0 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186106057 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186106057 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral\n$∬_(S)12xyzdS$\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z = x + y$ for $0 \\le x \\le 1$ and $0 \\le y \\le 1$." 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186106057 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}12xyz\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186106057 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}12xyz\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,12xy(x+y)\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,12xy(x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,12{x}^{2}y+12x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12{x}^{2}y+12x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\,[2{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(2+2)-0 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186106062 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186106062 1071 "![](images/78/7836860694ae6246dee36a70b86bc7fcd8cf7358d47a10586ea6880759fb1588.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n$∬_(S)(x^{2} + y^{2})dS$,\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z = 2x - y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above." 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186106062 1071 "![](images/78/7836860694ae6246dee36a70b86bc7fcd8cf7358d47a10586ea6880759fb1588.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2x-y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186106062 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186106063 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186106063 1071 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x, y, z) =-3xz$, where the surface $S$ is defined by $z = \\sqrt{2}x + \\sqrt{2}y$ for $0 \\le x \\le 1$ and $0 \\le y \\le 2$.\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below.\n$∬_(S)f(x, y, z)dS = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}AAAAAAAdydx$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186106063 1071 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=-3xz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=\\sqrt{2}x+\\sqrt{2}y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186106063 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{2}x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2}x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,-3x(\\sqrt{2}x+\\sqrt{2}y)\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,-3\\sqrt{10}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186106534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186106534 1071 "The point $(4, 2)$ is stretched with invariant $y$-axis and stretch factor $2$, and then stretched with invariant $x$-axis and stretch factor $-3$. What is the resulting point?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186106534 1071 "The point $(4,2)$ is stretched with invariant $y$-axis and stretch factor $2,$ and then stretched with invariant $x$-axis and stretch factor $-3.$ What is the resulting point?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186106534 1074 "Let's apply the given transformation.\n\n- A stretch of factor $2$ and invariant $y$-axis can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(2x,y).\n$$\n\n- A stretch of factor $-3$ and invariant $x$-axis can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(x,-3y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the combination of the stretches is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(2x,-3y).\n$$\n\nApplying the combined stretch to our point, we obtain\n\n$$\n(4,2)↦(2\\cdot 4,\\,-3\\cdot 2)=(8,-6).\n$$\n\n![](images/ab/ab3fda7652a99346f6d33e11b43d3401e2a427ab6d2f54c0f8717733fb3f892c.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186107117 1064 "We can combine stretches in the $x$-direction (with invariant $y$-axis) and stretches in the $y$-direction (with invariant $x$-axis).\n\nFor example, let's consider a combined stretch transformation that performs stretches in the following sequence:\n\n- a stretch in the $x$-direction with stretch factor $\\dfrac{1}{2}$, followed by\n- a stretch in the $y$-direction with stretch factor $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$.\n\nThe functional representation of the combined stretch is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}x, -\\dfrac{1}{2}y\\right)\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, it does not matter which stretch we perform first. But it's often helpful to think of the stretches being applied one at a time.\n\nLet's apply our combined stretch transformation to the polygon below.\n\n![](images/9b/9b0338a600efa76b14397a4d60b6de500daf4e136b2233b6fa4f021f0861fc24.png)\n\nTo stretch a polygon, we\n\n- find the images of the vertices, and then\n- draw segments connecting the image vertices.\n\nSo, our three vertices $(4,8)$, $(8,-4)$, $(-8,-8)$ are mapped to the following points by the stretch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(4, 8) & ↦ (\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4,-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 8) = (2,-4) \\\\ (8,-4) & ↦ (\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 8,-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (-4)) = (4, 2) \\\\ (-8,-8) & ↦ (\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (-8),-\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (-8)) = (-4, 4)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following result:\n\n![](images/ec/ecc35921e58a7a307fe941825209e0009ff7a1bdb42fc252c2b7ef6db7876e75.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186107117 1064 "We can combine stretches in the $x$-direction (with invariant $y$-axis) and stretches in the $y$-direction (with invariant $x$-axis).\n\nFor example, let's consider a combined stretch transformation that performs stretches in the following sequence:\n\n- a stretch in the $x$-direction with stretch factor $\\dfrac{1}{2},$ followed by\n\n- a stretch in the $y$-direction with stretch factor $-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe functional representation of the combined stretch is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\mapsto \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}x, -\\dfrac{1}{2}y\\right).\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, it does not matter which stretch we perform first. But it's often helpful to think of the stretches being applied one at a time.\n\nLet's apply our combined stretch transformation to the polygon below.\n\n![](images/9b/9b0338a600efa76b14397a4d60b6de500daf4e136b2233b6fa4f021f0861fc24.png)\n\nTo stretch a polygon, we \n\n- find the images of the vertices, and then\n\n- draw segments connecting the image vertices.\n\nSo, our three vertices $(4,8)$, $(8,-4)$, $(-8,-8)$ are mapped to the following points by the stretch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4,8) & ↦(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8)=(2,-4) \\\\ (8,-4) & ↦(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4))=(4,2) \\\\ (-8,-8) & ↦(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-8),\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-8))=(-4,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following result:\n\n![](images/ec/ecc35921e58a7a307fe941825209e0009ff7a1bdb42fc252c2b7ef6db7876e75.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186115121 1071 "![](images/70/70dc1594c0072b83429fc1f5d6be4fa454f6650c3eeef37c41c2d3ec691bb35f.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $y=x^2$ and the horizontal line $y=-1$ from $x=0$ to $x=1$, as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $360^{\\circ}$about the line $y=-1$." 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186115121 1071 "![](images/70/70dc1594c0072b83429fc1f5d6be4fa454f6650c3eeef37c41c2d3ec691bb35f.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $y=x^2$ and the horizontal line $y=-1$ from $x=0$ to $x=1,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $360^{\\circ}$about the line $y=-1.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186115121 1074 "First, note that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/3b/3b0427e7d37245d86d347d6d4f867ae3d17e5f0a030feb53d28ad4bbc856f85c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-1$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x^2$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} V &= \\pi \\int_{0}^{1} \\big[\\: \\left({\\color{blue}\\textrm{upper function}}\\right) - \\left({\\color{brown}\\textrm{lower function}}\\right) \\:\\big]^2\\,\\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= \\pi \\int_{0}^{1} \\left[\\: {\\color{blue}x^2}- \\left({\\color{brown}-1} \\right)\\:\\right]^2\\,\\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= \\pi\\int_{0}^{1} (x^2+1)^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= \\pi\\int_{0}^{1} (x^4+2x^2+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= \\left.\\pi \\left(\\dfrac{x^5}{5}+\\dfrac{2x^3}{3}+x \\right) \\Bigg\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\pi \\left(\\left[ \\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{2}{3}+1 \\right] - 0 \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{28 \\pi}{15}. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186115121 1074 "First, note that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/3b/3b0427e7d37245d86d347d6d4f867ae3d17e5f0a030feb53d28ad4bbc856f85c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=x^2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\tV &= \\pi \\int_{0}^{1} \\big[\\: \\left({\\color{blue}\\textrm{upper function}}\\right) - \\left({\\color{brown}\\textrm{lower function}}\\right) \\:\\big]^2\\,\\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\t \t&= \\pi \\int_{0}^{1} \\left[\\: {\\color{blue}x^2 }- \\left({\\color{brown}-1} \\right)\\:\\right]^2\\,\\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]  \n                &= \\pi\\int_{0}^{1} (x^2+1)^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\pi\\int_{0}^{1} (x^4+2x^2+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{x^5}{5}+\\dfrac{2x^3}{3}+x \\right) \\Bigg|_{0}^{1} \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\pi \\left( \\left[ \\dfrac{1}{5}+\\dfrac{2}{3}+1 \\right] - 0 \\right) \\[5pt]\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{28 \\pi}{15}.\n}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186115122 1071 "![](images/e9/e99f6cd10a72c91e5c7430a9ee18a45ba4dc09f941c779077567436a5fe90405.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x-1}$, the vertical line $x=1$, and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=1$, as shown in the picture. Find an integral expression for the volume of the resulting solid." 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186115122 1071 "![](images/e9/e99f6cd10a72c91e5c7430a9ee18a45ba4dc09f941c779077567436a5fe90405.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x-1},$ the vertical line $x=1,$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=1,$ as shown in the picture. Find an integral expression for the volume of the resulting solid." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186115122 1074 "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y$:\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{x-1} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=y^3+1\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.\n\n![](images/73/73fc04f5a8a56b0faba6ec2a77f8aafd864c84786adfe7da9ccda99d0c8a4d8f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=1$, and\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=y^3+1$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(right function) - (left function)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(y^{3} + 1) - (1)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}(y^{3})^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}y^{6}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186115122 1074 "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{x-1}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx=y^3+1\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/73/73fc04f5a8a56b0faba6ec2a77f8aafd864c84786adfe7da9ccda99d0c8a4d8f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=y^3+1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,({y}^{3}+1)-(1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{({y}^{3})}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{6}\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186115126 1071 "![](images/40/401368d830825a55e27fc6ba191a1c3dbb80d93b2e0af74db3337523817d2c65.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\dfrac 3 2x+\\dfrac 1 2$, for $x\\in [1,3]$, and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $y=1$, as shown above. Find an integral that represents the volume of the resulting solid." 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186115126 1071 "![](images/40/401368d830825a55e27fc6ba191a1c3dbb80d93b2e0af74db3337523817d2c65.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\dfrac 3 2x+\\dfrac 1 2,$ for $x\\in [1,3],$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $y=1,$ as shown above. Find an integral that represents the volume of the resulting solid." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186115126 1074 "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=1$ to $x=3$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/04/0434e16502f9b20bb53f3c46ba265239c59e8f5a487cdf3e71962fc0019a927d.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=1$, and\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\dfrac 3 2x+\\dfrac 1 2$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{1}^{3}[(upper function) - (lower function)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{1}^{3}[(\\frac{3}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}) - (1)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{1}^{3}(\\frac{3}{2}x - \\frac{1}{2})^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186115126 1074 "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=1$ to $x=3$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/04/0434e16502f9b20bb53f3c46ba265239c59e8f5a487cdf3e71962fc0019a927d.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\dfrac 3 2x+\\dfrac 1 2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\,(\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2})-(1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{(\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186115127 1071 "Consider the region bounded by the curve $x=\\sqrt{y}$, the vertical line $x=2$, and the horizontal line $y=4$, as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=2$.\n\n![](images/a5/a56f30e5912629c9854e68d4837a37dae55a005e98ef6cf5379b5eb81022afb7.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186115127 1071 "Consider the region bounded by the curve $x=\\sqrt{y},$ the vertical line $x=2,$ and the horizontal line $y=4,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=2.$\n\n![](images/a5/a56f30e5912629c9854e68d4837a37dae55a005e98ef6cf5379b5eb81022afb7.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186115127 1074 "We want to determine the limits of integration. To do so, we find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=2$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{y} &= 2 \\\\\ny &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=4$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.\n\n![](images/07/0748c87aa004f06f99a19ab4b7a2dadcf2f86920b53d1edd7ff19aced1d76f04.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=\\sqrt{y}$, and\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2$.\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{0}^{4}[(right function) - (left function)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{4}[(2) - (\\sqrt{y})]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{4}[2 - \\sqrt{y}]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{4}(4 - 4\\sqrt{y} + y)dy \\\\\n&= π[4y - \\frac{8}{3}y^{3/2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{4} \\\\\n&= π[(4(4) - \\frac{8}{3}(4)^{3/2} + ((4)^{2})/(2)) - (0)] \\\\\n&= π[16 - \\frac{64}{3} + 8] \\\\\n&= \\frac{8π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 false] [17592186115127 1074 "We want to determine the limits of integration. To do so, we find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=2$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =2 \\\\ y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=4$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is th