#edb/ref 17592186534671 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534966 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534966 1077 "field-2" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534966 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534671 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534966 1079 #uuid "f62681d9-cd8d-595e-ba4f-a4722ad371ab" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534301 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534319 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534334 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534483 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534967 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534566 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534967 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534967 1077 "selection" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534967 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534334 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534967 1079 #uuid "fa5bd8e0-2ab6-5b5d-9ae0-1984ba0dd1de" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534968 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534789 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534968 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534968 1077 "field-1" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534968 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534789 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534968 1079 #uuid "fb5a8c81-2643-51d7-9805-3937d851aee1" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534969 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534304 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534969 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534326 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534969 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534352 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534969 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534701 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534969 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534706 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534969 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534969 1077 "selection" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534969 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534352 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534969 1079 #uuid "ff7570d2-23c8-593e-ae05-f7eefecf996a" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534854 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534970 1069 "q-100626" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534970 1071 "If $-16{log}_{7}⁡(x-128)=-48$, then $x=$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534970 1072 #uuid "00bde4d7-2128-56ee-87f4-eb818e9cb4e3" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534970 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16{log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =-48 \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =\\frac{-48}{-16} \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(x-128) & =3 \\\\ {7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x-128)} & ={7}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(x-128)}=x-128.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-128 & ={7}^{3} \\\\ x-128 & =343 \\\\ x & =471.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534971 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534845 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534971 1069 "q-84641" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534971 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534971 1071 "![](images/11/11b00ddb6eb89e6acf79a8d5dc32e07a90850b97d8802df293e1240f6cfe444a.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534971 1072 #uuid "01f21d28-bd9a-5f49-bee9-224def342dbf" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534971 1074 "![](images/cd/cd63145c6f0513fe78da148b07064eb725c2de88ded4993dabccc0da173d6583.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})}=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}}=\\frac{4\\sqrt{7}}{7}.\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534920 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534972 1069 "q-120810" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534972 1071 "![](images/7e/7eb8812e90e4862a3658773186fe3809b212d58d5d22613fac6d9067ae4ccbc9.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ the vertical line $x=e,$ and the horizontal line $y=0$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=e,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following integrals gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534972 1072 #uuid "059b810f-7faa-5870-a600-8d8d6445cd02" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534972 1074 "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,⟹\\,x={e}^{y}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/f0/f0ddb5b21bdff9cbed426abed5bf9b94c4c4c4d742e2eb90dcdb92895c2adf3a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x={e}^{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=e.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(e)-({e}^{y})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(e-{e}^{y})^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2y}-2{e}^{y+1}+{e}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534829 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534973 1069 "q-353068" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534973 1071 "![](images/54/5453dfc44e11cad377c1e8e2c30fc23d537d4423d38fab4428395362f51d9412.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x={y}^{2}+1,$ the vertical line $x=5,$ and the horizontal line $y=0,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=5.$\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534973 1072 #uuid "08dd2009-a210-5f29-bbaa-81488a5664f6" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534973 1074 "We want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersection of the curve and the line $x=5$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+1 & =5 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=2$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/e8/e83c5729ef5db920a0b451a8571ad84f45d2b4e950ab831ced0da2c0a12cea50.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x={y}^{2}+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,5-({y}^{2}+1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(16-8{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(16y-\\frac{8}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi (32-\\frac{64}{3}+\\frac{32}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534950 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534974 1069 "q-90840" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534974 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}-\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{y}{x}\\,dy+dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534974 1072 #uuid "08e87b9c-9adc-5a8c-b060-dda37adbb915" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534974 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{y}{x}\\,dy+dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+\\frac{y}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{y}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =2(\\pi +1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534936 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534975 1069 "q-122473" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534975 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨12{t}^{2},-{e}^{-t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(-1,-4)$ and has velocity $⟨0,1⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534975 1072 #uuid "0ad91671-88c2-5e5d-8e1f-e5e0e6fbe489" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534975 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨12{t}^{2},-{e}^{-t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 12{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (-{e}^{-t})\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3}+{C}_{1},{e}^{-t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨0,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨4({0}^{3})+{C}_{1},{e}^{-0}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,1⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨4{t}^{3},{e}^{-t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4{t}^{3},{e}^{-t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{4}+{C}_{3},-{e}^{-t}+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-1,-4⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{0}^{4}+{C}_{3},-{e}^{-0}+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,-4⟩ & =⟨{C}_{3},-1+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=-1$ and ${C}_{4}=-3.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{4}-1,-{e}^{-t}-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534943 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534976 1069 "q-342471" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534976 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{5},$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | $5{x}^{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534976 1072 #uuid "0bc32c07-1675-5c17-a61b-a851fe6dbaf3" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534976 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{({x}^{5})}^{2}}\\cdot {({x}^{5})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{10}}\\cdot 5{x}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{1-{x}^{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534855 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534977 1069 "q-86058" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534977 1071 "Consider the region bounded by the curve $x=\\sqrt[3]{y},$ the vertical line $x=3,$ and the horizontal lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=3.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534977 1072 #uuid "0ccddc8e-6343-5b87-aa99-4a041586cf69" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534977 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/47/47b612a3e08ff24055cbffa7d53fb337f1905ec861abd3a8e535088d383162cb.png)\n\nNotice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/19/1927874f5eca0c18b7b61b37d0e6fac5198ec9dcf89205763e88570287330292.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=\\sqrt[3]{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(3)-(\\sqrt[3]{y})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[3-\\sqrt[3]{y}]}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2/3}-6{y}^{1/3}+9)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}y-6{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}y+9{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{3{y}^{5/3}}{5}{|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{9{y}^{4/3}}{2}|}_{0}^{1}+9y{|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{3}{5}-0)-(\\frac{9}{2}-0)+(9-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{51\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534842 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534978 1069 "q-216060" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534978 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a+\\frac{3}{5}b=\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}a+\\frac{1}{7}b=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534978 1072 #uuid "0f1e5457-cea5-5c5a-997b-b02818578f46" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534978 1074 "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $5$ and the second equation by $7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a+\\frac{3}{5}b=\\frac{9}{5} & |\\times 5 \\\\ \\frac{3}{7}a+\\frac{1}{7}b=1 & |\\times 7\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5a+3b=9 \\\\ 3a+b=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the second equation can easily be solved for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+b & =7 \\\\ b & =7-3a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $b=7-3a$ into the first equation and then solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a+3b & =9 \\\\ 5a+3(7-3a) & =9 \\\\ 5a+21-9a & =9 \\\\ -4a & =-12 \\\\ a & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $b,$ we substitute $a=3$ into the equation $b=7-3a$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =7-3a \\\\  & =7-3(3) \\\\  & =7-9 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $a=3$ and $b=-2.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534965 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534979 1069 "q-14951" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534979 1071 "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534979 1072 #uuid "0f1fd02c-d7d8-5411-85bd-473d05c64e92" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534979 1074 "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n}\\cdot n}{(9+5n)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{9+5n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\sqrt[n]{n}\\cdot \\frac{5n}{n})}{(\\frac{9}{n}+\\frac{5n}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(\\frac{9}{n}+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 1}{0+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534900 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534980 1069 "q-64521" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534980 1071 "![](images/2b/2b7a0fe3a80bfa6a0e0d82ab46a94740ce4b73007ae9d17ceb83170bce4559ff.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{6}.$ Rounded to two decimal places, what is the measure of the angle $\\theta ?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534980 1072 #uuid "132171d1-1085-585d-b8f2-7700ff87a0d2" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534980 1074 "![](images/ea/eaf0e0a6434b454b58a8f53de0445bff8bae14d8bd1ec9e8e9598f0bd80323d9.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe compute the angle $\\theta $ using the inverse cosine:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{6})=(80.405\\,93…)^{∘}≈{80.41}^{∘}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534921 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534981 1069 "q-124837" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534981 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534981 1072 #uuid "198d2d03-309c-5424-a889-3994cff0e1c9" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534981 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }25{cos}^{2}⁡t-25{sin}^{2}⁡t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }25\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{e}^{2t}\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534916 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534982 1069 "q-267390" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534982 1071 "![](images/8f/8feb78ada73af4c97e7ce60555cf22acf5e24da38e30424c4c5c430e6456c06d.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534982 1072 #uuid "1ba23b8d-4b13-599f-bc74-567006cafd11" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534982 1074 "![](images/41/41c6badfbcafe7bafdddca30606511d2d6c3c8bdbbcd5ec2e29ff9c4c2dd310f.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534923 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534983 1069 "q-161328" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534983 1071 "![](images/78/7836860694ae6246dee36a70b86bc7fcd8cf7358d47a10586ea6880759fb1588.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+\\sqrt{8}y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534983 1072 #uuid "1bae08c0-8385-58de-b3f9-2f81e17da99b" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534983 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534957 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534984 1069 "q-45448" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534984 1071 "![](images/9f/9fd4e55e5de09555b5a1fe05f8fb5f47b03d3b0cd9d96cbdca526f0d357d0afb.png)\n\nA stretch of factor $3$ in the $x$-direction and $2$ in the $y$-direction is applied to the polygon shown above. Which of the following is the resulting polygon?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534984 1072 #uuid "1c9a7b3c-46d7-5715-a15a-49321cace888" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534984 1074 "A stretch of factor $3$ in the $x$-direction and $2$ in the $y$-direction can be represented by the following function:\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(3x,2y)\n$$\n\nTo stretch a polygon, we \n\n- find the images of the vertices, and then\n\n- draw segments connecting the image vertices.\n\nSo, our three vertices $(1,3)$, $(1,-2)$, $(-2,2)$ are mapped to the following points by the stretch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1,3) & ↦(3\\cdot 1,2\\cdot 3)=(3,6) \\\\ (1,-2) & ↦(3\\cdot 1,2\\cdot (-2))=(3,-4) \\\\ (-2,2) & ↦(3\\cdot (-2),2\\cdot 2)=(-6,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following result:\n\n![](images/6c/6c77ca7fa6efa27125c131c629b5afeba784b8ca4fd1039530879c2ec177b8dc.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534918 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534985 1069 "q-104972" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534985 1071 "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-2y & 4y-2x \\\\ 4y-2x & 4x\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(1,1)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point of $f$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534985 1072 #uuid "22b41189-2cbf-5d3c-9c30-272b9cf4d5e7" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534985 1074 "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=(-2)\\cdot 4-{2}^{2}=-12<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=-2<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,1)<0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534828 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534986 1069 "q-50022" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534986 1071 "![](images/10/1066e10a5a9860e371ca2895ec39f7cdce4e000d5cca9f3b478f57cd890d647a.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ for $x\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],$ and the horizontal line $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534986 1072 #uuid "236b0649-a813-5e30-b589-a9199c88af15" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534986 1074 "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/6d/6d4c086c9a3a99609728bc7c53f5a28ac86431e5c37e2b87bcfd3031a32aa470.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{1}{x})-(\\frac{1}{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534963 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534987 1069 "q-315696" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534987 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534987 1071 "Maya runs a lemonade stand. The table below gives the selling rate $r(t)$ (in liters per hour) over a $6$-hour period starting at noon.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)\\,(\\text{L/hour})$ | $1$ | $k$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters sold over the $6$-hour period (with three subintervals defined by the given points) is $28$ liters, then what is the value of $k?$ \n\n$\\,\\,k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534987 1072 #uuid "247e80ec-6b94-56ac-94f4-78dcaa13b56f" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534987 1074 "The area $A$ under the curve $y=r(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{r({t}_{0})+r({t}_{1})+…+r({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(r(0)+r(2)+r(4))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $r(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $5$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+k+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(k+6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of liters sold by Maya. Since we are given that the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters sold is $28,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+12 & =28 \\\\ 2k & =16 \\\\ k & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534988 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534861 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534988 1069 "q-120906" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534988 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534988 1071 "![](images/14/144259aa150936de0e3425c13f953da0833d87c2aba1304aa39b3c47fda03550.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $x=6\\sqrt{y}+1,$ the horizontal line $y=1,$  the horizontal line $y=0,$ and the vertical line $x=-1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-1$, as shown above. Find the volume of the resulting solid." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534988 1072 #uuid "2588c644-3085-550f-abbf-182631d5c4e8" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534988 1074 "Notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left function and which is the right, let's draw our horizontal arrow.\n\n![](images/8a/8a17502c300fae56e9f2e1706d23a3e5a7d3233eebff9a77958d117a0135adb0.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=6\\sqrt{y}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the resulting solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(6\\sqrt{y}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(6\\sqrt{y}+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(36y+24\\sqrt{y}+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(36y+24{y}^{1/2}+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(36\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+24\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2}+4y)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(18(1)^{2}+16(1)^{3/2}+4(1))-0] \\\\  & =38\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534884 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534989 1069 "q-120837" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534989 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534989 1071 "![](images/68/68359143b50b4d6187f2d50793b1d3cb4b48656f1996cd222cb5d07f666b535e.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=-1-x,$ the vertical line $x=-3,$ and the horizontal line $y=0$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-3,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534989 1072 #uuid "27279421-a932-5375-bd1a-4824ccac1d67" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534989 1074 "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=-1-x\\,⟹\\,x=-1-y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=-3$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1-y & =-3 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=2$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/73/73d5da7e1bd4b409574f6e9ab034dde51012eb956b3ea72a3529f7d432fe781d.png)\n\n \n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=-3,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=-1-y.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,(-1-y)-(-3)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2-y)}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534944 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534990 1069 "q-64981" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534990 1071 "![](images/cb/cbff0c974c790ec4287be1dcff8aa62d0da0aa5e77fa5ec577633f5f2cad6a5d.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534990 1072 #uuid "288e585e-09b6-54a4-9e8e-a55aaefc8005" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534990 1074 "![](images/1f/1ffb0a83abf7e736c47035f4f337c36a6bb129fd0d64fe0ac3bfe8e46c194a11.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534838 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534852 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534922 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534991 1069 "q-346698" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534991 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534991 1071 "Consider the area under the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=1.$\n\nThe estimated area under the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,5]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534991 1072 #uuid "2a6ffc0a-296f-5d33-ab74-d79dec06ff68" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534991 1074 "![](images/67/671d5efc8abc559cf638bfbfdda76bddb0b8d5c2d9134dffbbf7eab0e2f0b25a.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $7$ | $17$ | $31$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+7+17+31)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =56\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{increasing}$ over the interval $[1,5],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534868 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534992 1069 "q-353061" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534992 1071 "![](images/9d/9d8f2d52a0d943a4eeb1d323cc90db145c9b87b57e87d595a7ca5809498e7a6a.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x=y+1,$ the vertical line $x=4,$ and the horizontal line $y=0,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=4.$\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534992 1072 #uuid "2f10ec35-272f-563f-8af5-2c0fc2e74c5d" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534992 1074 "We want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersection of the curve and the line $x=4$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =4 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=3$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/f9/f9fd1773b9599b1a4b61fc58c854bf998ed147a9707f719a80a3588874c0eb4f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=y+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}[(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}[4-(y+1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3-y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}(3-y)^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0-{3}^{3}) \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534853 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534906 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534993 1069 "q-215740" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534993 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534993 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{1}{3}y=6 \\\\ 5x-y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534993 1072 #uuid "2f8f1100-44e9-5de0-ac27-77c13ead7f67" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534993 1074 "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $3.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{1}{3}y=6 & |\\,\\times 3 \\\\ 5x-y=4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+y=18 \\\\ 5x-y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $y$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-y$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $y$-variable, and allows us to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+\\cancel{y} & =18 \\\\ 5x-\\cancel{y} & =4 \\\\ 11x & =22 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $x=2$ into the equation $6x+y=18$ to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+y & =18 \\\\ 6\\cdot 2+y & =18 \\\\ 12+y & =18 \\\\ y & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=2,\\,y=6.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534994 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534951 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534994 1069 "q-6027" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534994 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534994 1071 "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}.$ Does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534994 1072 #uuid "30cdb164-81fa-5575-aede-39cb02771ca3" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534994 1074 "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{11}{7\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{n!}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{7}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{n!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{7}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{n!}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince $L=0<1,$ the series converges by the root test." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534835 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534995 1069 "q-120535" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534995 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534995 1071 "![](images/8f/8fa5e5b2300c3395d48f5355352cd6ece7477f3bae8e8c185f0f869fa197b0e9.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x$ and the horizontal line $y=2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534995 1072 #uuid "31757f7a-27f2-5cad-90a3-e9168497ad54" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534995 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-2)+(x-2) & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/5a/5a1821ff500c901d9f072fe56101f17e579018d0710543c4a2e371214894725c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(2)-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2-x+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534841 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534996 1069 "q-124877" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534996 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534996 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534996 1072 #uuid "34262fe5-7916-5312-b653-79d60bb7bbe7" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534996 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534945 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534997 1069 "q-111997" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534997 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534997 1071 "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $16{y}^{2}-81=0.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534997 1072 #uuid "36228bc3-c8df-58ee-9fb1-b8814e29ddd8" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534997 1074 "Using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{y}^{2}-81 & =0 \\\\ (4y)^{2}-(9)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (4y-9)(4y+9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n4y-9=0\\,\\text{or}\\,4y+9=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-9 & =0 & \\, & \\, & 4y+9 & =0 \\\\ 4y & =9 & \\, & \\, & 4y & =-9 \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{4} & \\, & \\, & y & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-\\frac{9}{4},\\frac{9}{4}.$ We can write this even more concisely as $y=\\pm \\frac{9}{4}.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534948 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534998 1069 "q-6022" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534998 1071 "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}.$ Does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534998 1072 #uuid "3a25fcb4-6a85-5416-8ab8-d6107a3d8b17" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534998 1074 "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\cdot {n}^{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{n}^{n-1/2}} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{3}}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=0<1,$ the series converges by the root test." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534917 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534966 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534968 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534999 1069 "q-321990" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534999 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534999 1071 "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534999 1072 #uuid "3d2f4bec-f2bc-5622-80f4-e5a5ebaa94bf" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186534999 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{6}{5}(x+2) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+3 & =\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{6}{5}(y+3) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+{(y+3)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+2)}^{2}+{(y+3)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{36}.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534927 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535000 1069 "q-125496" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535000 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535000 1071 "![](images/a5/a5f23af5dd514a65937eee29bb0992ff94d68897d8c6422fcf215aad45379c4a.png)\n\nConsider the part of parabola and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the part of parabola is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535000 1072 #uuid "3f935fec-3b1f-581f-b980-6cf035478e31" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535000 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works with the particle for $y<0,$ while $F$  works against the particle for $y>0.$ \n\n![](images/53/537aca6190fc2f3e06e85c0781a8bb461559ef0a7515ee29aca505f9d615f820.png)\n\n \n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $y<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $y>0.$ So,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/49/4939f9a22400a7a56eaeacb77d520e522f50aa8378ca1014a19810122e12da51.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535001 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534891 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535001 1069 "q-352955" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535001 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535001 1071 "![](images/0e/0e402d36061211a9e51df852f7fa595eb212e5c6ce9b3d43a2d53f9bea1fedd3.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=(x-1)^{4}$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=1.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535001 1072 #uuid "3fd041ce-cfe7-560a-8362-3b96b6047662" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535001 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{4} & =1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/2a/2a2e4be26d18e408bf6e4580c72ec2f3c08eed1750d38e0716f06a32d3c03e7c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=(x-1)^{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,1-(x-1)^{4}\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-2(x-1)^{4}+(x-1)^{8})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(x-\\frac{2}{5}(x-1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{9}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi ([2-\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(1)^{9}\\text{ }]-[0-\\frac{2}{5}(-1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(-1)^{9}\\text{ }]) \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi }{45}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534926 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535002 1069 "q-353047" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535002 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535002 1071 "The region enclosed by the curve $y=4-(x-1)^{2},$ the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and the horizontal line $y=5$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=5.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535002 1072 #uuid "4017e457-d6e9-5799-a313-b509dcbd530f" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535002 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/ad/ad089d39428498123469a2ac7895f7d04b8b3ff9dac18a1698462bfc7fabae99.png)\n\nNotice that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/97/975f68d06bb52e162ad792987899509f4090c03ec1b55af6335982a2951f6bbf.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4-(x-1)^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=5.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,5-(4-(x-1)^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(1+(x-1)^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(1+2(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}((x-1)^{4}+2(x-1)^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}(x-1)^{5}+\\frac{2}{3}(x-1)^{3}+x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}+2-(-\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{3}+0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{43}{15}+\\frac{13}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{56\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534913 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535003 1069 "q-342666" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535003 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535003 1071 "$$\n\nCalculate ${g}^{′}(x)$ for $g(x)={e}^{x}\\,\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(4x+3).$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $={e}^{x}\\,\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(4x+3)\\,+$ | $4{e}^{x}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | {{field-1}} |" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535003 1072 #uuid "40534571-9b68-5a71-b2eb-96fad07c5254" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535003 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAnd the product rule is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}(u\\cdot v)}{\\text{d}x}={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, chain rule, and  formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(4x+3)+{e}^{x}\\cdot {(\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(4x+3))}^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(4x+3)+{e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(4x+3)^{2}+1}}\\cdot {(4x+3)}^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(4x+3)+{e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(4x+3)^{2}+1}}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\,\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(4x+3)+\\frac{4{e}^{x}}{\\sqrt{(4x+3)^{2}+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535004 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534954 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535004 1069 "q-15857" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535004 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535004 1071 "![](images/33/33070b4b871552c4c45f21f0885cbce45ff65eb278cb000e68c91adfc2ab004c.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $16$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(16,0)?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535004 1072 #uuid "41e7831f-c290-5b73-a0b9-fe7067c749d0" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535004 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=16.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534848 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535005 1069 "q-37891" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535005 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535005 1071 "![](images/5f/5f64637e42c8117ac66501a06470aa805ae3cf15fddf7840317394db9acb470f.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{6},$ which of the following is correct?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535005 1072 #uuid "47def0cf-3ef3-5e5d-b5dc-5aca43c3b9ae" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535005 1074 "![](images/a9/a935d38f0306fe713c997a2904267356359fdcd8f14ca5b655e0c420a5756708.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{5}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534912 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535006 1069 "q-81661" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535006 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535006 1071 "![](images/9d/9d0b7f238afe0f6cb82bb8670b9589aa10490b6e776e7f4a991a561aa0ad2245.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-x}$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ from $x=0$ to $x=3,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated ${360}^{∘}$about the line $y=1.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535006 1072 #uuid "48038fbf-e0e6-55ac-98d1-e8c48a8b376a" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535006 1074 "First, note that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=3$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/73/73fc1e2cad0969a629e2c2c4a55ce8ac66c16b67fe2984e8b75dc6aa3a5eeace.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\sqrt{4-x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,(\\sqrt{4-x})-(1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}((4-x)-2\\sqrt{4-x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(5-x-2\\sqrt{4-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi (5{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{4-x}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\pi (5x{|}_{0}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{3}+\\frac{4}{3}(4-x)^{3/2}{|}_{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi ((15-0)-(\\frac{9}{2}-0)+\\frac{4}{3}(1-8)) \\\\  & =\\pi (15-\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{28}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534878 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535007 1069 "q-86288" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535007 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535007 1071 "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[1,10]$ with a regular step size. Estimate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[1,10]$ using a left Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $2$ |" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535007 1072 #uuid "4bbaa29d-7536-57fa-abdb-fc58099b0936" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535007 1074 "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(1)+f(4)+f(7))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the interval $[1,10]$ is divided into $3$ equal subintervals, the width of each subinterval is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{10-1}{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the left endpoints:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+2+0)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534919 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535008 1069 "q-120816" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535008 1071 "![](images/5d/5d154a914d58f5b695adec9dd873ac067ba8280a4fefffd4f2f5559ff5ee04da.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{1}{1-x},$ the vertical line $x=2,$ and the horizontal line $y=-2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=2,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following integrals gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535008 1072 #uuid "4fe06b9c-2dd8-5751-a283-a97db5419aa5" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535008 1074 "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{1-x}\\,⟹\\,x=1-\\frac{1}{y}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=-2$ to $y=-1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/23/239113c4d2bbca26c694796f6f17bd26bebf47697dad53f7af832088213fd4c9.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=1-\\frac{1}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{[\\,(2)-(1-\\frac{1}{y})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{(1+\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534969 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535009 1069 "q-88610" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535009 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535009 1071 "What function represents a stretch of factor $4$ and invariant $x$-axis, followed by a stretch of factor $3$ and invariant $y$-axis?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535009 1072 #uuid "501956c6-823d-5f9c-8694-d92497158079" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535009 1074 "- A stretch of factor $4$ and invariant $x$-axis can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(x,4y).\n$$\n\n- A stretch of factor $3$ and invariant $y$-axis can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(3x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the combination of the stretches is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(3x,4y).\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534889 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535010 1069 "q-364487" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535010 1071 "Estimate the area $A$ under the curve $y=4{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,3]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535010 1072 #uuid "52c6a4cd-b903-5d26-bd7b-6413b0fd0ae1" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535010 1074 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/27/275b62fbec1c1a550cb23b481ec54c1e12d3d42e70cf95cd917f1b932638add4.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5))\\cdot 0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-1$ at each left endpoint:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $8$ | $15$ | $24$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+8+15+24)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534876 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534938 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535011 1069 "q-335435" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535011 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(-2) \\\\ y(-2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535011 1072 #uuid "530f8a57-b248-5ddf-8ae9-a8b808f3a729" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535011 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t+2)}\\,x(-2) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2)) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2))\\, \\\\ \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535012 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534832 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535012 1069 "q-106297" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535012 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535012 1071 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535012 1072 #uuid "58130b5b-5f37-553e-8991-9f3dd2a84005" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535012 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535013 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534894 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535013 1069 "q-7220" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535013 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535013 1071 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨2,2⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(-10,-8)$ and has velocity $⟨0,0⟩$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535013 1072 #uuid "581fcb98-ffbb-541a-98a8-b1aad8f2210c" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535013 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector. To find the velocity vector, we first need to integrate the acceleration vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2,2⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t+{C}_{1},\\,2t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(3)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨2(3)+{C}_{1},\\,2(3)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨6+{C}_{1},6+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-6$ and ${C}_{2}=-6.$ Therefore, the velocity vector is\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2t-6,\\,2t-6⟩.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the position vector by integrating the velocity vector.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2t-6,\\,2t-6⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (2t-6)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (2t-6)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-6t+{C}_{3},\\,{t}^{2}-6t+{C}_{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(3)=⟨-10,-8⟩.$ Substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(3) & =⟨(3)^{2}-6(3)+{C}_{3},\\,(3)^{2}-6(3)+{C}_{4}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-10,-8⟩ & =⟨-9+{C}_{3},\\,-9+{C}_{4}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{3}=-1$ and ${C}_{4}=1.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-6t-1,\\,{t}^{2}-6t+1⟩.\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534905 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535014 1069 "q-6891" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535014 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535014 1071 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535014 1072 #uuid "5bcab4b1-53e9-547b-81a4-3bf0881c104a" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535014 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =16(1) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534940 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535015 1069 "q-150013" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535015 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535015 1071 "![](images/dc/dcf7a2ea5b44f3f5ba7b1663f7979cdc3fc8c1036b6cc797904ffda77f1003bd.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the сurve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535015 1072 #uuid "5c3b3b24-43f3-512c-b4aa-af0e4524c252" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535015 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/81/818ad935fd7aa592f00c23c9c77799d127449b4bb6ffacee4354e7acf39887d8.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/70/70e268a08195a92573f41927ae7de653837fca273f0c75cb951243a31ec660a2.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534850 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534893 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535016 1069 "q-321957" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535016 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535016 1071 "![](images/ca/ca3a73d411d7b766196a2bebf56f9d678aba54a4f2265ac08c36d0fb3fbf03b0.png)\n\nThe arc of the circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535016 1072 #uuid "5e3d47e1-6723-5134-bd1d-2937862c014c" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535016 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/37/37665aa7ea5388a1162ca68a1662aaa86d6369f083d7563dbf27c521c13a7c72.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534839 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535017 1069 "q-50007" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535017 1071 "![](images/84/848b73762d79b0b7b898f3c011e4118256b5fca37fa9d702313bc16da04b4c3d.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=2{x}^{2},$ for $x\\in [0,1],$ and the horizontal line $y=2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535017 1072 #uuid "5f36b991-1cba-5c10-9333-13a2ade76e0a" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535017 1074 "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/3b/3b634be23e06f6038e00b91f27eadacd1d1bcc51516d741786cc433a1ce731ce.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(2)-(2{x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{x}^{4}-8{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534860 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534866 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535018 1069 "q-215834" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535018 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535018 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+2y=1 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{y}{9}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535018 1072 #uuid "634cc981-026f-5720-a0d6-757e4b38cffe" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535018 1074 "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $9.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+2y=1 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{y}{9}=-1 & |\\,\\times 9\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+2y=1 \\\\ 6x-y=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $y$ in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-y & =-9 \\\\ 6x+9 & =y \\\\ y & =6x+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $y=6x+9$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2y & =1 \\\\ 5x+2(6x+9) & =1 \\\\ 5x+12x+18 & =1 \\\\ 17x+18 & =1 \\\\ 17x & =-17 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $y,$ we substitute $x=-1$ into the equation $y=6x+9,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6x+9 \\\\  & =6(-1)+9 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1,\\,y=3.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535019 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534851 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535019 1069 "q-45450" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535019 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535019 1071 "![](images/76/7625ed8002bbd684ba004185f357e8b8e9b3384857684ef836ccd4a019a317cd.png)\n\nThe stretch $S(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{2}x,\\frac{1}{3}y)$ is applied to the polygon shown above. Which of the following is the resulting polygon?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535019 1072 #uuid "63c2d1f3-bff7-58f3-9c73-044cb81121d6" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535019 1074 "To stretch a polygon, we \n\n- find the images of the vertices, and then\n\n- draw segments connecting the image vertices.\n\nSo, our three vertices $(4,6)$, $(5,-3)$, $(-6,-6)$ are mapped to the following points by the stretch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4,6) & ↦(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4,\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6)=(2,2) \\\\ (6,-3) & ↦(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6,\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3))=(3,-1) \\\\ (-6,-6) & ↦(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-6),\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6))=(-3,-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following result:\n\n![](images/aa/aa9f0b7c785c5c830c0e902f500fe0998df4c4ee8ace07af6989cf9aad2a88ea.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534887 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535020 1069 "q-85996" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535020 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535020 1071 "The region enclosed by the curve $y={e}^{x},$ the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=1,$ and the horizontal line $y=3$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $y=3.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535020 1072 #uuid "6b9b18aa-2c78-5504-8e8f-08d14250da87" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535020 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/72/72b701d2404e1acdcfba6b1994588419afc8246de6df8bf50e93baca0051df6a.png)\n\nNotice that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/89/89d6b6d2d65f161353da5fea9175aaf780f0fdc38405b855fbbc033349a484db.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={e}^{x},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=3.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(3)-({e}^{x})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{(3-{e}^{x})}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(9-6{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(9x-6{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi ([9-6e+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\text{ }]-[-6+\\frac{1}{2}]) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{29}{2}-6e+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(29-12e+{e}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534928 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535021 1069 "q-44216" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535021 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535021 1071 "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $100{x}^{2}=7.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535021 1072 #uuid "6bcd8b40-daaf-5baf-a297-367e7c76cde5" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535021 1074 "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100{x}^{2} & =7 \\\\ 100{x}^{2}-7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100{x}^{2}-7 & =0 \\\\ (10x)^{2}-{(\\sqrt{7})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (10x-\\sqrt{7})(10x+\\sqrt{7}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n10x-\\sqrt{7}=0\\,\\text{or}\\,10x+\\sqrt{7}=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x-\\sqrt{7} & =0 & \\, & \\, & 10x+\\sqrt{7} & =0 \\\\ 10x & =\\sqrt{7} & \\, & \\, & 10x & =-\\sqrt{7} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10} & \\, & \\, & x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10},\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}.$ We can write this even more concisely as $x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{10}.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535022 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534856 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535022 1069 "q-74506" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535022 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535022 1071 "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[-3,5].$\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[-3,5].$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535022 1072 #uuid "6dc2cb61-7e14-5e92-9670-4b835f928971" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535022 1074 "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈f(-3)Δ{x}_{1}+f(-1)Δ{x}_{2}+f(0)Δ{x}_{3}+f(3)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $2$ | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈-1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2+1+6+8 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535023 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534947 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535023 1069 "q-124870" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535023 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535023 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,i+j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ]?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535023 1072 #uuid "6f3c55a9-3e7c-5106-8273-98cee0c05243" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535023 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y(t)=1.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+({sin}^{2}⁡(2t)-{1}^{2})\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535024 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534873 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535024 1069 "q-81786" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535024 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535024 1071 "![](images/46/4643824b623d5325f53407147f086a95073db91be0148381780668c420ec7c45.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x={e}^{y},$ the vertical line $x=e,$ and the horizontal line $y=0,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=e.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535024 1072 #uuid "6ffaec2e-3741-5b7e-88f5-bdff517c9f5d" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535024 1074 "We want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=e$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y} & =e \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/93/93fd0875ea2ba1d1d92bb781a61cfb114ac88b7d240cd825b4842f686f7c760f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x={e}^{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=e.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(e)-({e}^{y})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[e-{e}^{y}]}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2}-2{e}^{y+1}+{e}^{2y})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi ({e}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y+1}\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\pi ({e}^{2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-2{e}^{y+1}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{{e}^{2y}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =\\pi (({e}^{2}-0)-2({e}^{2}-e)+(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\pi (-{e}^{2}+2e+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4e-{e}^{2}-1)\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(-1+4e-{e}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535025 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534892 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535025 1069 "q-19890" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535025 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535025 1071 "A speedboat travels downstream on a river. The table below gives the speed $v(t)$, in miles per hour, of the boat at certain moments during its journey.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)\\,(\\text{mi/hour})$ | $10$ | $35$ | $40$ | $65$ | $80$ |\n\nUse a left Riemann sum for $v(t)$ over the interval $[0,2]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total distance traveled by the speedboat.\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the speed-time graph.*" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535025 1072 #uuid "725799d2-b847-5f90-a1b3-5a3cdfe95070" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535025 1074 "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=2,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{2-0}{4}=0.5.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{v({x}_{0})+v({t}_{1})+…+v({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(v(0)+v(0.5)+v(1)+v(1.5))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $v(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)$ | $10$ | $35$ | $40$ | $65$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(10+35+40+65)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =150\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance traveled by the speedboat. So, the distance is $75\\,\\text{mi}.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535026 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534840 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535026 1069 "q-341959" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535026 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535026 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=4\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(3x-4),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535026 1072 #uuid "727d2976-e027-5f8e-a059-0575427eb806" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535026 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(4\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(3x-4)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(3x-4)) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(3x-4)^{2}-1}}\\cdot (3x-4)^{′} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(3x-4)^{2}-1}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{(3x-4)^{2}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535027 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534897 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535027 1069 "q-49103" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535027 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535027 1071 "Daniel decides to go on a diet. The table below gives the rate $w(t)$, in pounds per week, at which he loses weight over a period of $7$ weeks.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{week})$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $w(t)\\,(\\text{pounds/week})$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n\nUse a left Riemann sum for $w(t)$ over the interval $[0,7]$ with three subintervals to approximate Daniel's total weight loss." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535027 1072 #uuid "7303a5f4-0c27-5036-a2ee-f35a42950785" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535027 1074 "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈w(0)Δ{t}_{1}+w(1)Δ{t}_{2}+w(3)Δ{t}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i+1}-{t}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $-$ |\n| $w(t)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1+4+4 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total weight lost by Daniel. He lost approximately $9$ pounds." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535028 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534909 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535028 1069 "q-88767" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535028 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535028 1071 "The point $(5,-5)$ is stretched with invariant $y$-axis and stretch factor $2,$ and then stretched with invariant $x$-axis and stretch factor $2.$ What is the resulting point?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535028 1072 #uuid "758a989a-3043-5362-867a-7a0673f17cc3" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535028 1074 "Let's apply the given transformation.\n\n- A stretch of factor $2$ and invariant $y$-axis can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(2x,y).\n$$\n\n- A stretch of factor $2$ and invariant $x$-axis can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(x,2y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the combination of the stretches is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(2x,2y).\n$$\n\nApplying the combined stretch to our point, we obtain\n\n$$\n(5,-5)↦(2\\cdot 5,\\,2\\cdot (-5))=(10,-10).\n$$\n\n![](images/8e/8e3eccf9b9190db3c19e2d49d633763d420947daa12929a8516fcf066465165d.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535029 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534883 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535029 1069 "q-51130" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535029 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535029 1071 "The following table shows the speed $v$, in $\\text{km/min},$ at certain times during a family car trip over a period of $60$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{min})$ | $60$ | $70$ | $80$ | $95$ | $110$ | $120$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v\\,(\\text{km/min})$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1.5$ |\n\nAssuming that the family traveled $130\\,\\text{km}$ during the first hour of the trip, use the left Riemann sum to approximate the total distance traveled during the first two hours.\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the speed-time graph.*" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535029 1072 #uuid "79154f2c-c86a-5c07-9c5a-51aae4f7090e" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535029 1074 "In this case, we have $n=5$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈v(60)Δ{t}_{1}+v(70)Δ{t}_{2}+v(80)Δ{t}_{3}+v(95)Δ{t}_{4}+v(110)Δ{t}_{5},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i+1}-{t}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $60$ | $70$ | $80$ | $95$ | $110$ | $120$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $10$ | $10$ | $15$ | $15$ | $10$ | $-$ |\n| $v(t)$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1.5$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈10\\cdot 1.5+10\\cdot 2+15\\cdot 1+15\\cdot 1+10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =15+20+15+15+20 \\\\  & =85\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the given context, the area under the curve represents the distance covered in $60$ minutes of travel, from $t=60$ minutes to $t=120$ minutes. The distance traveled in the first two hours is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d=130+85=215\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535030 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534880 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535030 1069 "q-3720" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535030 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535030 1071 "![](images/6a/6a225129d0879af950e935d794c09f3b479434154757a643c29c44a9d9e981e2.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $\\overset{―}{DE}?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535030 1072 #uuid "7b6428c3-ddc3-50c7-8b44-d4c45bc81ecd" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535030 1074 "From the figure, we note that the points $D$ and $E$ have coordinates ${{(-4,0)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and ${{(0,3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$  respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{DE},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DE & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-(-4))^{2}+(3-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534830 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534930 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535031 1069 "q-321945" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535031 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535031 1071 "![](images/79/795d51d5695c48f5d12c9057030f6a48e58a579fb262a83afc132425ec7668b3.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535031 1072 #uuid "7d806c10-53ec-5b39-8560-fe07bced1d84" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535031 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/a7/a78203eda3869c4478a97da0875dda7e72717911a6e1aee524bb963533a5e12e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}].\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535032 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534879 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535032 1069 "q-20953" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535032 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535032 1071 "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1$ on the interval $[-2,\\frac{1}{2}].$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535032 1072 #uuid "7df7ee25-2f51-5474-8055-7ad2776c92c8" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535032 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1,$ we get\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}+6x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}+6x & =0 \\\\ 6x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-1,0,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,\\frac{1}{2}$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =2(-2)^{3}+3(-2)^{2}+1=-3 \\\\ f(-1) & =2(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+1=2 \\\\ f(0) & =2(0)^{3}+3(0)^{2}+1=1 \\\\ f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}+3{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=f(\\frac{1}{2})=2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-2)=-3,$ so the global minimum is $-3.$\n\n![](images/b7/b7741f6f67f6070d6050ebaf792598593f98a406bdab91f73356e2c27085b7a0.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535033 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534886 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535033 1069 "q-344385" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535033 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535033 1071 "The exact global minimum value of the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}$ on the closed interval $-3\\le x\\le 5$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535033 1072 #uuid "7e7a47fb-69a2-506b-bf8c-6f05366124e0" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535033 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+4}}.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all real values of $x$ in the interval. So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=-3,5$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =\\sqrt{-3+4}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ f(5) & =\\sqrt{5+4}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(5)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-3)=1,$ so the global minimum is $1.$\n\n![](images/a6/a638683e712156f829a3d6562de44c6c72b7fb6d2c360fa293b3dbaea7297ea9.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535034 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534895 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535034 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534932 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535034 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534956 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535034 1069 "q-346697" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535034 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535034 1071 "Consider the area under the curve $y=4x+1$ over the interval $[0,1]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.25.$\n\nThe estimated area under the curve $y=4x+1$ over the interval $[0,1]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535034 1072 #uuid "7e9c9afd-571f-54f0-b5de-d6a5e5729d5e" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535034 1074 "![](images/e8/e8cd8966d69260c77685d349c97eceb1545686d347f6cdb827ad470880797bf5.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(0.25)+f(0.5)+f(0.75)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+2+3+4)\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =10\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{increasing}$ over the interval $[0,1],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535035 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534907 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535035 1069 "q-102954" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535035 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535035 1071 "![](images/0b/0b0ed011d85165937593e0cc86439f0d26ab61e703c5c74ac824e10a5cfa4adf.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535035 1072 #uuid "813ed141-a0ad-5559-9d01-1e28afeea83d" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535035 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- The graph lies in the first and third quadrants\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain.\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\n- As $x\\to -\\infty $, we have $y\\to -\\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nOf the given options, only graph IV has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535036 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534958 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535036 1069 "q-86298" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535036 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535036 1071 "The following table shows the rate of change of a city's temperature over a $10$-hour period, starting at $2\\,\\text{a.m}.$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{h})$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T\\,{(}^{∘}\\text{C}/\\text{h})$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ | $2$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the total change in the temperature from $2\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $12\\,\\text{p.m}$ has a value of $26{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ what is the value of $k?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535036 1072 #uuid "84844930-f95f-5f3a-8037-4adc201a50fc" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535036 1074 "The area $A$ under the curve $y=T(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{T({t}_{0})+T({t}_{1})+…+T({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(T(2)+T(4)+T(6)+T(8)+T(10))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $T(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T(t)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+k+4+2+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+10) \\\\  & =2k+20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total change in the temperature from $2\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $12\\,\\text{p.m}.$ Since we are given that the left Riemann sum approximation of the total change in the temperature is $26{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+20 & =26 \\\\ 2k & =6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535037 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534834 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535037 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534931 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535037 1069 "q-277907" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535037 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535037 1071 "In **ascending order**, the roots of $f(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}-4t-21}{{t}^{2}+7t+10}$ are\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$\nand\n$\\,\\,t=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535037 1072 #uuid "860910b4-2f3e-5abd-a759-661586cb6055" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535037 1074 "The roots of $f(t)$ are the solutions to the equation $f(t)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{t}^{2}-4t-21}{{t}^{2}+7t+10}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor $f(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}-4t-21}{{t}^{2}+7t+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{(t-7)(t+3)}{(t+5)(t+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-7)(t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =7,\\,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $f(t)=0$, in ascending order, are $t=-3$ and $t=7.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534858 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534935 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535038 1069 "q-216038" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535038 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535038 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}p-\\frac{1}{4}q=3 \\\\ -4p-7q=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,q=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535038 1072 #uuid "87b4272d-ed6b-52ae-b49c-9dfe670380e7" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535038 1074 "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $12.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}p-\\frac{1}{4}q=3 & |\\,\\times 12 \\\\ -4p-7q=4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4p-3q=36 \\\\ -4p-7q=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $4p$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-4p$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $p$-variable, and allows us to solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{4p}-3q & =36 \\\\ -\\cancel{4p}-7q & =4 \\\\ -10q & =40 \\\\ q & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $q=-4$ into the equation $-4p-7q=4$ to solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4p-7q & =4 \\\\ -4p-7\\cdot (-4) & =4 \\\\ -4p+28 & =4 \\\\ -4p & =-24 \\\\ p & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $p=6$ and $q=-4.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535039 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534914 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535039 1069 "q-113732" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535039 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535039 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4}{{z}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535039 1072 #uuid "8a064553-8bcb-586c-b022-5e4a307bf7cb" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535039 1074 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{{z}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}z & =4\\int {z}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{{z}^{-4+1}}{-4+1})+C \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{{z}^{-3}}{-3})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {z}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3{z}^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535040 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534847 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535040 1069 "q-74503" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535040 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535040 1071 "Estimate the area under the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x+2$ over the interval $[1,3]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535040 1072 #uuid "8bc09582-a4d5-5866-bf69-244d81d8d845" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535040 1074 "![](images/2c/2cd664908ea218fecf5a33ea3312bd883f5495d64fc0da8387632a7d2e17c26a.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $1.25$ | $2$ | $3.25$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+1.25+2+3.25)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,3],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535041 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534960 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535041 1069 "q-89857" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535041 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535041 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$\n\nI. The graph of $f(x)$ lies in the second and fourth quadrants\n\nII. The range of $f(x)$ is $y\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty $" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535041 1072 #uuid "96f2566c-9717-5c2b-9e76-8782952293d7" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535041 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n![](images/ab/abafb7d55b4baa0b06f20c3de2b44d5925f7286f078aaa76d71aded7c9c9a001.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since the graph of $f(x)$ lies in the first and third quadrants\n\n- Statement II is false since the range of $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535042 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534859 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535042 1069 "q-342458" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535042 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535042 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=-\\sqrt{7}\\,\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\,x),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535042 1072 #uuid "973108cd-4a12-5f7e-8def-c33e7591ffaa" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535042 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\,x)) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\sqrt{3}\\,x)) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-{(\\sqrt{3}\\,x)}^{2}}\\cdot {(\\sqrt{3}\\,x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-3{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{3{x}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535043 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534964 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535043 1069 "q-216035" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535043 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535043 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{3}{2}y=-5 \\\\ -2x-y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535043 1072 #uuid "98e817d9-610e-538f-a711-341d85a62ad2" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535043 1074 "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $6.$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{3}{2}y=-5 & |\\,\\times 6 \\\\ -2x-y=-2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+9y=-30 \\\\ -2x-y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. Notice that the $2x$ term in the first equation can cancel with the $-2x$ term in the second equation.\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable, and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{2x}+9y & =-30 \\\\ -\\cancel{2x}-y & =-2 \\\\ 8y & =-32 \\\\ y & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=-4$ into the equation $-2x-y=-2$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-y & =-2 \\\\ -2x-(-4) & =-2 \\\\ -2x+4 & =-2 \\\\ -2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=3$ and $y=-4.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535044 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534863 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535044 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534961 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535044 1069 "q-351160" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535044 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535044 1071 "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $r=⟨0,0⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$r\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535044 1072 #uuid "990e6c9c-e46f-5a33-8086-74712d90c327" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535044 1074 "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{0}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,2+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}-2⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535045 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534867 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535045 1069 "q-315714" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535045 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535045 1071 "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[0,8].$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $5$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $6$ | $5$ | $9$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area $A$ under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[0,8].$ \n\n$A≈$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535045 1072 #uuid "991c1ace-0aaf-57b9-84aa-e45b1d07c8fd" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535045 1074 "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈f(0)Δ{x}_{1}+f(2)Δ{x}_{2}+f(5)Δ{x}_{3}+f(6)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $5$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $2$ | $3$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $6$ | $5$ | $9$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈3\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 3+6\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6+12+6+10 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535046 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534833 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535046 1069 "q-315694" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535046 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535046 1071 "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[2,8]$ with a regular step size.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $k$ | $1$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $18$, then what must be the value of $k?$ \n\n$\\,\\,k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535046 1072 #uuid "9be6577c-8d1d-5823-8ae8-eea53999e09f" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535046 1074 "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(2)+f(4)+f(6))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the points are $2,4,6,8$ with three subintervals, the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{8-2}{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each left-endpoint:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $k$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈((-3)+5+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(2+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A=18$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+2k & =18 \\\\ 2k & =14 \\\\ k & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535047 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534955 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535047 1069 "q-161354" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535047 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535047 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $x\\le y\\le x+1.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535047 1072 #uuid "9f471502-6ac3-5895-874a-d7c88c5897e2" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535047 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}+{0}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{x+1}{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}y]}_{y=x}^{y=x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}(1+{e}^{2x})^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((1+{e}^{2})^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535048 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534934 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535048 1069 "q-81172" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535048 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535048 1071 "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-4y-4x,$ and determine which of the following statements is true." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535048 1072 #uuid "9f714ecf-c225-5f6f-850c-327baa1dc6f6" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535048 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-4y-4x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-4y-4x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & 2(xy-2)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4=0 \\\\ xy-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these values into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny=-2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{-2}=-1,\\,\\,y=2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-4)=2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-4)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-1,-2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,-2)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-1,-2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,-2)=det({f}^{″}(-1,-2))=0\\cdot (-2)-(-4)^{2}=-16<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-1,-2).$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,2)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,2)=det({f}^{″}(1,2))=0\\cdot 2-{4}^{2}=-16<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2).$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535049 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534831 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535049 1069 "q-332061" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535049 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535049 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{6t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535049 1072 #uuid "a295f504-6868-5fd6-8219-b8135f071992" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535049 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{6t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the right." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535050 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534959 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535050 1069 "q-50020" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535050 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535050 1071 "![](images/d2/d27538da9cf9ebb39324477a9c3e9eabd950b648507ebc616a11d5c8a6054a19.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3},$ for $x\\in [0,2],$ and the horizontal line $y=-1$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $y=-1,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535050 1072 #uuid "a85d5623-8f4e-5539-b87a-051d9e911395" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535050 1074 "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/94/94d3f7e76db23c387366024bdd5dbb25f8adddc25d78bb3fc81f5900b4128fcf.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y={x}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,({x}^{3})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{6}+2{x}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535051 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534904 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535051 1069 "q-50081" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535051 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535051 1071 "![](images/42/421d588cd290294cf6471704f2459c22b00c8b47756b808e9da267a8007792b2.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=2,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535051 1072 #uuid "a978de85-c2b4-579a-92f4-5064fe99c547" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535051 1074 "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=2{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe take the positive sign since $x\\ge 0.$\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=2$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}} & =2 \\\\ y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=8$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/c7/c762c0e41cf8862535e5241a00149fdc6e409c3f79630a411cff73fcd11759b0.png)\n\n \n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}{[\\,(2)-(\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}{(\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}-2)}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}(\\frac{y}{2}-4\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}+4)\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535052 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534911 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535052 1069 "q-105916" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535052 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535052 1071 "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $\\frac{1}{3}$ centered at the origin?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535052 1072 #uuid "a981750b-2a73-53da-b2bb-9bf98660a4c5" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535052 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535053 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534875 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535053 1069 "q-2750" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535053 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535053 1071 "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the closed interval $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535053 1072 #uuid "aa173a50-134e-5fe9-8151-0ed518869f1b" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535053 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\pi ,$ since it is the only value that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\pi $ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=1 \\\\ f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\pi )=1,$ so the global maximum is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](images/7e/7e0008d35355afebc392a4bc508bd2cf2de5dc32a108f9f9c030d75a24ece076.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535054 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534908 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535054 1069 "q-81048" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535054 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535054 1071 "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535054 1072 #uuid "ac06f66c-0484-5c24-8d99-9d11ac2dbda8" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535054 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4x & e-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x=0 \\\\ e-{e}^{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-4x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second  equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ne-{e}^{y}=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$   \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(e-{e}^{y})=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(e-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -e\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))=(-4)\\cdot (-e)-{0}^{2}=4e>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,1))_{11}=-4<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,1).$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535055 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534888 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535055 1069 "q-139038" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535055 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535055 1071 "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535055 1072 #uuid "af92a5d6-20ee-53fa-be9c-28e2a23392fd" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535055 1074 "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{8n+1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{n(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 1}{8+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{5}{4}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535056 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534885 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535056 1069 "q-79167" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535056 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535056 1071 "![](images/15/158ce9317d062a090ed36bda7361d025e4c8f4c68ea43484f50015f01c411a30.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535056 1072 #uuid "b1d2632a-4694-5c22-b050-b15669bba222" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535056 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/5c/5cc99d78c5fe5afacde4ba96e2a4010b0fab66bdabbe5c2b518bd936f36c7d84.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}].\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535057 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534898 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535057 1069 "q-90834" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535057 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535057 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy-z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535057 1072 #uuid "b34fc554-6133-5c96-98c9-93a11d4b7862" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535057 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,z(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,k \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}-y\\,dx+x\\,dy-z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt-z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2t\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t-4t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9-4t\\,dt \\\\  & =(9t-2{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(9-\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534953 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535058 1069 "q-50017" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535058 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535058 1071 "![](images/29/293593a043a7713d87a2f8ad8a2eaecee26b34288e4662bb4b2fce834e703e56.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3},$ the vertical line $x=-1$ and the horizontal lines $y=0$ and $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-1,$ as shown above. Which of the following integrals gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535058 1072 #uuid "b4260f61-3124-52b4-9558-94f62f160010" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535058 1074 "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny={x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/60/600ef6d7265add5c669a29394e45facae040292ce19caad9dcae390979b604b9.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=\\sqrt[3]{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\sqrt[3]{y})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt[3]{y}+1)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2/3}+2{y}^{1/3}+1)\\,\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535059 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534925 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535059 1069 "q-74490" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535059 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535059 1071 "Estimate the area under the curve $y=3x-{x}^{2}$ over the interval $[0.5,2.5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535059 1072 #uuid "b62ef58e-043d-550b-83fb-b95a07d27915" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535059 1074 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/0e/0e3f499cbc36c9dca7e759b67a8fd9e94959039f3403832e1d60b9ed598c44dd.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1.25$ | $2$ | $2.25$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1.25+2+2.25+2)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535060 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534949 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535060 1069 "q-115695" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535060 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535060 1071 "Estimate the area under the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+3x+1$ over the interval $[1.5,3.5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535060 1072 #uuid "b8274948-1d4b-57b0-9024-c09c00401038" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535060 1074 "![](images/ad/ad1e85721eb2142f5cad422530aeed86c0919f34261d19e11e9f056cf355b387.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5)+f(3)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.25$ | $3$ | $2.25$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.25+3+2.25+1)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =9.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[1.5,3.5],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535061 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534937 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535061 1069 "q-344375" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535061 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535061 1071 "The global maximum value of the function $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x+1}$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le 8$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535061 1072 #uuid "b8cfbdaa-2d28-5714-8504-0797810897ae" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535061 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x+1},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all real values of $x$ in the interval. So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=0,8$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =2\\sqrt{0+1}=2 \\\\ f(8) & =2\\sqrt{8+1}=2\\sqrt{9}=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(8)=6,$ so the global maximum is $6.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=2,$ so the global minimum is $2.$\n\n![](images/de/def7a0e41c8534710eec8bfa3c0f6795b5fbb20ea09058403046be5bef4594fb.png)" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535062 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534846 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535062 1069 "q-89851" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535062 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535062 1071 "![](images/19/19c55ecaf73ac1c0c8db619e6c43d14f35e50e2321ccae5687b3a1bc6a687563.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535062 1072 #uuid "bb2d031b-f14f-51f1-86a8-2bb2273a329c" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535062 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n\n- The graph lies in the first and third quadrants\n\n- The function is increasing throughout its entire domain\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\n- As $x\\to -\\infty $, we have $y\\to -\\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nOf the given options, only graph I has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535063 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534874 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535063 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534901 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535063 1069 "q-277916" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535063 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535063 1071 "In **ascending order**, the roots of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-8}{{x}^{2}-9x+18}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$\nand\n$\\,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535063 1072 #uuid "bdbe7587-32bb-56f0-8c5c-492c8b22516f" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535063 1074 "The roots of $f(x)$ are the solutions to the equation $f(x)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-8}{{x}^{2}-9x+18}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2x-8}{{x}^{2}-9x+18} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+4)(x-2)}{(x-3)(x-6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $f(x)=0$, in ascending order, are $x=-4$ and $x=2.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535064 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534941 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535064 1069 "q-124387" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535064 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535064 1071 "![](images/c5/c5b92e2126df555db5f639bc9063586b6afc3bb2896b14edadedf183ec152ac5.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{1}{3})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535064 1072 #uuid "be261196-18fe-5211-8ddf-c96cd6bddf72" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535064 1074 "![](images/df/df5d54940a136a53c1e1c00f8cc9d71d7b064f6e0895a029da2738cf21998a56.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535065 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534915 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535065 1069 "q-334744" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535065 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535065 1071 "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{4}+0.5(Δx)^{5}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4}$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535065 1072 #uuid "c354aefb-fffc-5ecc-8cac-e2625bd63b47" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535065 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{4}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=11(Δx)^{4}+0.5(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $11(Δx)^{4}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.2Δx+6(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.2Δx.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535066 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534910 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535066 1069 "q-277942" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535066 1071 "Solve $f(t)=0,$ where $f(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}-5t+6}{3t-9}.$\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535066 1072 #uuid "c691641a-bf97-5ba0-ae7f-e25ca5480f64" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535066 1074 "First, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}-5t+6}{3t-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{(t-3)(t-2)}{3(t-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(t-3)}(t-2)}{3\\cancel{(t-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(t-2)}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t-2 & =0\\,⟹\\,t=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to $f(t)=0$ is $t=2.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534946 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535067 1069 "q-246761" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535067 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535067 1071 "![](images/1d/1d1cf52a210aba670c697863822f0534669127d178e8df13d4aecfe8adf01df8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle. Rounding to two significant figures, the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535067 1072 #uuid "c979c575-4198-50eb-b74a-9d92d0b93552" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535067 1074 "![](images/eb/eba7dd6d571c989a14900a91eb432a216b74717046bfaa19e5fbe4de7abe6503.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\theta ={20}^{∘}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{20}^{∘}=0.939\\,69…≈0.94.\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535068 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534902 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535068 1069 "q-216062" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535068 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535068 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}s-\\frac{2}{3}t=-1 \\\\ s+\\frac{1}{4}t=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535068 1072 #uuid "cb34e92b-11ce-5586-a6db-ed20acf39bd8" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535068 1074 "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}s-\\frac{2}{3}t=-1 & |\\times 3 \\\\ s+\\frac{1}{4}t=2 & |\\times 4\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}5s-2t=-3 \\\\ 4s+t=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the second equation can easily be solved for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4s+t & =8 \\\\ t & =8-4s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $t=8-4s$ into the first equation and then solve for $s:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5s-2t & =-3 \\\\ 5s-2(8-4s) & =-3 \\\\ 5s-16+8s & =-3 \\\\ 13s & =13 \\\\ s & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $t,$ we substitute $s=1$ into the equation $t=8-4s$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =8-4s \\\\  & =8-4(1) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $s=1$ and $t=4.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534903 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535069 1069 "q-14954" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535069 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535069 1071 "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535069 1072 #uuid "d497f3f8-01e4-568d-82c0-af0842ace04d" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535069 1074 "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{6}{5\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{{n}^{6}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[n]{n}})}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot {1}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{6}{5}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534967 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535070 1069 "q-79163" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535070 1071 "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(-5,-2)?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535070 1072 #uuid "d4ab0ec3-092c-5d3f-b9c2-e48440315561" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535070 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(-5,-2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534862 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535071 1069 "q-115676" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535071 1071 "A tank is filling with soda. The table below gives the rate $R(t)$, in liters per minute, at which the tank is filling at certain moments since it started.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)\\,(\\text{liters/minute})$ | $30$ | $40$ | $60$ | $70$ | $90$ |\n\nGiven that the tank is empty when $t=0,$ use a left Riemann sum for $R(t)$ over the interval $[0,12]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total volume of soda in the tank after $12$ minutes." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535071 1072 #uuid "d4c5ef62-20c9-5f14-af52-91dedd4c1fc7" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535071 1074 "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=12,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{12-0}{4}=3.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{R({t}_{0})+R({t}_{1})+…+R({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(R(0)+R(3)+R(6)+R(9))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $R(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $30$ | $40$ | $60$ | $70$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(30+40+60+70)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =200\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total volume in the tank. So, the volume is $600$ liters." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534933 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535072 1069 "q-124866" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535072 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535072 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+yz\\,dy+z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the line $r(t)=t\\,i+5t\\,j+\\,k$ for $t\\in [2,3]?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535072 1072 #uuid "d6062a1b-c189-5825-831a-1185f156e120" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535072 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=5t,\\,z(t)=1.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+5\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+yz\\,dy+z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}xy\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+yz\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}(xy\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+yz\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}5{t}^{2}\\cdot (1)+5t\\cdot (5)+1\\cdot (0)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}5{t}^{2}+25t\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534881 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535073 1069 "q-50514" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535073 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535073 1071 "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨2t-2,2t⟩\\,,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position  $r=⟨-4,-8⟩$ when $t=1,$ find the times when the particle is at the origin." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535073 1072 #uuid "d9799b8b-4af9-5ece-b36d-864fdc8c4517" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535073 1074 "The particle is at the origin when its position is $r=⟨0,0⟩.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨2t-2,2t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (2t-2)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-2t+{C}_{1},\\,{t}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-4,\\,-8⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨(1)^{2}-2(1)+{C}_{1},\\,(1)^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4,\\,-8⟩ & =⟨-1+{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-3$ and ${C}_{2}=-9.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨{t}^{2}-2t-3,\\,{t}^{2}-9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the origin, we solve the equation $r(t)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}^{2}-2t-3,\\,{t}^{2}-9⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-2t-3=(t-3)(t+1) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-1,3, \\\\ {t}^{2}-9=(t+3)(t-3) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=3.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534843 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535074 1069 "q-88759" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535074 1071 "What function represents a stretch of factor $5$ and invariant $x$-axis, followed by a stretch of factor $2$ and invariant $y$-axis?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535074 1072 #uuid "dd1b71b5-3284-5639-806c-2226f712ef21" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535074 1074 "- A stretch of factor $5$ and invariant $x$-axis can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(x,5y).\n$$\n\n- A stretch of factor $2$ and invariant $y$-axis can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(2x,y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the combination of the stretches is given by\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(2x,5y).\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534836 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535075 1069 "q-161350" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535075 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535075 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{z}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=y-3x$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $3x\\le y\\le 3.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535075 1072 #uuid "e16a799c-84bb-5d3d-b669-6d70bdf08db4" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535075 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-3x)=-3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-3x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{z}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\sqrt{1+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{3x}^{3}\\sqrt{y-3x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{2}{3}(y-3x)^{3/2}]}_{y=3x}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-3x)^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{3}\\,{[\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})(3-3x)^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{45}(0-{3}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{45}(9\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{33}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535076 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534869 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535076 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534924 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535076 1069 "q-351222" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535076 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535076 1071 "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv(t)=⟨6t,\\,4t⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position  $r=⟨-12,\\,-8⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the times when the particle is at the origin. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the particle is at the origin when $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535076 1072 #uuid "e49b201b-78f0-5668-9145-a0f21b5a41c8" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535076 1074 "The particle is at the origin when its position is $r=⟨0,0⟩.$ So first, we need to compute the position function of the particle.\n\nTo compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨6t,\\,4t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 6t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2{t}^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨-12,-8⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨3(0)^{2}+{C}_{1},\\,2(0)^{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-12,-8⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=-12$ and ${C}_{2}=-8.$ Therefore, the position of $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨3{t}^{2}-12,\\,2{t}^{2}-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is at the origin, we solve the equation $r(t)=⟨0,0⟩.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3{t}^{2}-12,\\,2{t}^{2}-8⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-12 & =3(t-2)(t+2)=0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 2, \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-8 & =2(t-2)(t+2)=0\\,⇒\\,t=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the particle is at the origin when $t=-2$ and $t=2.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534864 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535077 1069 "q-49890" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535077 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535077 1071 "![](images/28/2817662752008c41b76d1beb9bec73907f55d836524fe48c583997670421f70b.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=2{x}^{2},$ for $x\\in [-2,\\frac{5}{2}],$ and the horizontal line $y=-1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-1,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535077 1072 #uuid "e80b3506-231f-529b-b14d-6bb0a866165f" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535077 1074 "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=-2$ to $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/e1/e1774466f020515d3296f2e48fda0a61154bddbfd2285a67c97bdc7e35b60da5.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}[\\,(2{x}^{2})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}(4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535078 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534929 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535078 1069 "q-125502" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535078 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535078 1071 "![](images/8a/8aed400e7768b70f4532642418d83b36d9e32aa84db87c71ea6d902504930bc4.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535078 1072 #uuid "e8382e90-f8cf-5c02-a1ba-6dd0a94124ee" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535078 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/d8/d8e05de34bf9ddfdbf9580b1df32eb9e091f56f76ac3616cbb1cd81d5d212a86.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/17/17423916013677c1476df5a4368ab9eec81671b04b4ca29a0e702b20ddc818f2.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534877 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535079 1069 "q-125694" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535079 1071 "![](images/3c/3c2035d50b823b9bcbeb3ae8cf698df9a25c95a289c5a6f35054269dc920088d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535079 1072 #uuid "e8988e8d-780c-5cf1-8b3a-d358392fe42f" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535079 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/df/dfec76b47e8508a57e18440a72986bcae8e2bd19559bd0915ea4530e0b08b4f6.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/58/58e3efc39d0aee02d14e7c80c7355c37a1f28b6f0f54ff7102553e3a6ffc17cf.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534882 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535080 1069 "q-71866" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535080 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535080 1071 "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[0,10].$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $5$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[0,10].$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535080 1072 #uuid "e9dcd5b8-f37b-55de-ab7a-6d8ec569a890" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535080 1074 "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈f(0)Δ{x}_{1}+f(3)Δ{x}_{2}+f(5)Δ{x}_{3}+f(7)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $3$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $5$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 2+9\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3+8+10+27 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535081 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534871 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535081 1069 "q-216177" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535081 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535081 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}x+2y=-6 \\\\ -x+\\frac{1}{3}y=-10\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535081 1072 #uuid "ecc873f4-57df-56ae-b5fa-d73b11c36cc7" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535081 1074 "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $4$ and the second equation by $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}x+2y=-6 & |\\times 4 \\\\ -x+\\frac{1}{3}y=-10 & |\\times 3\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+8y=-24 \\\\ -3x+y=-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. We can eliminate $x$ by adding the two equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{3x}+8y & =-24 \\\\ -\\cancel{3x}+y & =-30 \\\\ (8y+y) & =(-24-30) \\\\ 9y & =-54 \\\\ y & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=-6$ into the first equation to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+8y & =-24 \\\\ 3x+8(-6) & =-24 \\\\ 3x-48 & =-24 \\\\ 3x & =24 \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=8,\\,y=-6.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535082 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534872 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535082 1069 "q-81681" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535082 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535082 1071 "![](images/c2/c21d4794aeedcc70fe037a936724d2708a484d43c457b2d120927f86f1fa6ffc.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x-2$ and the horizontal line $y=-2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-2.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535082 1072 #uuid "f0334985-4e7c-56a4-ba8b-035c69ca7733" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535082 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-2 & =-2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/e7/e785138362c91f9034d90ee9d923fc818cd299b3ee5d6d25eb1bf40cabc2f2b0.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-2x-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,(-2)-({x}^{2}-2x-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+2x)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi ([\\frac{1}{5}(2)^{5}-(2)^{4}+\\frac{4}{3}(2)^{3}\\text{ }]-[0]) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{32}{5}-16+\\frac{32}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535083 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534952 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535083 1069 "q-115688" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535083 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535083 1071 "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[-3,3]$ with a regular step size. If the left Riemann sum approximation of the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $4$, then what must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ | $5$ |" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535083 1072 #uuid "f3cd5193-df6c-5672-95df-52f56264b423" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535083 1074 "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(-3)+f(-1)+f(1))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+k+4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(5+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A$ must be $4$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10+2k & =4 \\\\ 2k & =-6 \\\\ k & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534844 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535084 1069 "q-161327" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535084 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535084 1071 "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x+\\sqrt{2}y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535084 1072 #uuid "f409435d-6032-5802-bf1f-c20613731f7b" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535084 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{2}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(1)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2(x+\\sqrt{2}y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,2({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}xy)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535085 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534942 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535085 1069 "q-12786" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535085 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535085 1071 "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $p$ is $4{p}^{2}=25.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535085 1072 #uuid "f49a62d5-679a-524a-aa83-c1365bd71520" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535085 1074 "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{p}^{2} & =25 \\\\ 4{p}^{2}-25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{p}^{2}-25 & =0 \\\\ (2p)^{2}-{5}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (2p-5)(2p+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n2p-5=0\\,\\text{or}\\,2p+5=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p-5 & =0 & \\, & \\, & 2p+5 & =0 \\\\ 2p & =5 & \\, & \\, & 2p & =-5 \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{2} & \\, & \\, & p & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $p=-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5}{2}.$ We can write this even more concisely as $p=\\pm \\frac{5}{2}.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535086 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534849 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535086 1069 "q-79162" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535086 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535086 1071 "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-2,3)?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535086 1072 #uuid "f4ba96fb-f5da-576a-bf60-04d5aed6b6b9" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535086 1074 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-2,3).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534896 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534899 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534939 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534962 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535087 1069 "q-331796" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535087 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535087 1071 "Consider a function $z(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{z(t)\\}=Z(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{6t}z(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{6t}z(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535087 1072 #uuid "f860a2b3-6e2b-5a0c-99c8-165c277a9c9f" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535087 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{6t}z(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{6t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-6)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $Z(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-6)t}\\,z(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Z(s-6),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-6>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+6.\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535088 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534865 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535088 1069 "q-113731" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535088 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535088 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{4}{3}{y}^{7}\\,\\text{d}y.$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535088 1072 #uuid "fc2dfa06-fbfe-5d00-98fd-cc43b27c052d" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535088 1074 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{3}{y}^{7}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\int {y}^{7}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(\\frac{{y}^{7+1}}{7+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{8}}{8}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{8}}{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534837 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535089 1069 "q-96426" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535089 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535089 1071 "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(2x-1),$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535089 1072 #uuid "fd040bd9-e3ef-5708-a352-cc4c61514e23" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535089 1074 "$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(2x-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x-1)^{2}-1}}\\cdot (2x-1)^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(4{x}^{2}-4x+1)-1}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-4x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4x(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x(x-1)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534890 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535090 1069 "q-86296" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535090 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535090 1071 "John is selling juice at a market stall. The table below gives the rate $r(t)$ of juice sold over a period of $6$ hours, from $1\\,\\text{p.m.}$ to $7\\,\\text{p.m.}$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)\\,(\\text{liters/hour})$ | $k$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters John sold during the given period has a value of $16$ liters, what is the value of $k?$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535090 1072 #uuid "fd327109-8d69-58f0-a86a-7b3b70080707" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535090 1074 "The area $A$ under the curve $y=r(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{r({t}_{0})+r({t}_{1})+…+r({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(r(1)+r(3)+r(5))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $r(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)$ | $k$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+2+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+5) \\\\  & =2k+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of liters sold by John. Since we are given that the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters sold is $16,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+10 & =16 \\\\ 2k & =6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535091 1062 #uuid "003358a9-f2af-59ba-b62d-17d3096b53b8" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535091 1063 #edb/ref 17592186482539 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535092 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535096 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535092 1062 #uuid "01dd852f-2929-5c79-b2c7-9966a69ca50b" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535092 1063 #edb/ref 17592186311561 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535093 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535108 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535093 1062 #uuid "055f6147-a057-5f62-8e99-02d25958931d" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535093 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069298 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535094 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535141 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535094 1062 #uuid "06beea17-9258-5f04-8cd8-47ee4a7a192b" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535094 1063 #edb/ref 17592186535039 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535095 1062 #uuid "114b601e-a2a6-516f-83df-76e39dceef0a" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535095 1063 #edb/ref 17592186535055 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535096 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535127 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535096 1062 #uuid "1241689c-e056-52b5-b26f-3355c23157a3" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535096 1063 #edb/ref 17592186535011 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535097 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535116 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535097 1062 #uuid "197c6117-5c00-5077-8365-1a9c9af6dfe0" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535097 1063 #edb/ref 17592186535073 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535098 1062 #uuid "1c02ef41-09d7-5c72-a8d1-2766d66724c0" 13194139535868 17592186045417 true] [17592186535098 1063 #edb/ref 17592186534970 13194139535868 17